window.CONTENT = window.CONTENT || {};
window.CONTENT.papers = [{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 01","total":22,"wordbank":["asking","benefit","compare","copy","equally","focus","forbid","general","hinder","obviously","quickly","recall","struggle","useful","wandering"],"materials":{"cloze":"Many students now type their lecture notes on a laptop, yet a growing number of teachers encourage them to write by hand. One reason is speed: typing allows a student to record almost every word, but this can actually (26) ____ understanding. When people write slowly, they must decide what matters and express it in their own words, which makes the material easier to (27) ____ later. A study at an American university found that students who took notes by hand performed better on questions that required them to explain ideas, although the two groups scored (28) ____ on simple factual questions. Handwriting also seems to keep the mind from (29) ____ during long lectures, because the writer cannot simply copy words without thinking. Of course, laptops remain (30) ____ for courses with large amounts of data, and no teacher should (31) ____ students who genuinely need them. The (32) ____ message is that the method matters less than the thinking behind it. Teachers can help by (33) ____ fewer but better questions and by giving students time after class to (34) ____ their notes with a partner. In the end, good notes are not a (35) ____ of the lecture but a record of the student's own ideas.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Every year thousands of books are left in the green boxes outside the charity's office. Some come from families whose children have grown up; others are sent by schools replacing their library stock, and a few publishers donate damaged but readable copies. A small van collects the boxes twice a week and takes them to a warehouse on the edge of the city. Last year alone the charity received more than ninety thousand books, which is both a wonderful problem and a serious one, because every single copy has to be handled."},{"key":"B","text":"Inside the warehouse, volunteers work in teams of six. One group checks each book for missing pages, water damage and writing on the pages, and anything beyond repair goes to a recycling centre. Another group sorts the rest into categories: picture books, novels, science, history and reference. A third group stamps the charity's name inside the cover. On a busy Saturday the teams can process about four thousand books, which sounds impressive until you remember how many are waiting."},{"key":"C","text":"Before a box is sent anywhere, staff contact the school that will receive it. They ask the head teacher how many students there are, what ages they teach and which subjects need materials. A school with no laboratory may ask for science books, while one in a rural area may simply want stories to encourage reading in English. Sending the wrong books wastes money and disappoints children, so the charity keeps a list of requests and matches each donation to a school."},{"key":"D","text":"Getting the boxes to the schools is the hardest part. Lorries can reach towns on main roads, but many schools lie at the end of dirt tracks that become mud in the rainy season. In those places the boxes are transferred to motorbikes, which carry two at a time up the hills. Drivers sometimes wait for days for the weather to improve. The charity now stores books in regional depots so that the final journey is short, even if the first one is long."},{"key":"E","text":"Books alone do not create readers. Before a delivery, two trainers spend a week with the teachers, showing them how to run a reading club, how to read aloud with expression and how to let children choose books for themselves. The teachers are also given simple record cards so that they can see which pupils are reading and which are not. Most schools ask for the training again the following year, and some have started their own libraries with the skills they learned."},{"key":"F","text":"The results are easiest to see in the children's faces. In one mountain school, a girl of ten told a volunteer that she had never owned a book before and asked whether she could keep it. Teachers report that pupils who join the reading club attend school more regularly and write longer stories. Numbers cannot measure everything, but the charity's own survey found that reading confidence had risen sharply in almost every school it visited."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"For most of the twentieth century, the university library was a quiet building whose main purpose was to keep books safe. Students came in, found a title in the catalogue, read it at a table and left. The librarian's job was to protect the collection and to make sure that nothing was lost.\n\nThat model has changed almost beyond recognition. A modern university library is more likely to be judged by how many students use it than by how many volumes it owns. Many libraries have moved the shelves to make room for group study areas, computer rooms and small studios where students can record videos or practise presentations. Some have opened cafés, and a few even lend equipment such as cameras, laptops and musical instruments.\n\nThe change has not been welcomed by everyone. Older academics sometimes complain that a library without silent reading rooms is no longer a library at all, and that students who work in groups distract those who need quiet. Librarians reply that they have simply followed the students.\n\nSurveys at several universities show that most students still use the building, but for a wider range of activities than their parents did. They borrow fewer printed books, yet they ask more questions, attend more workshops and use more online resources. The most successful libraries, according to a recent report, are those that offer both worlds: a silent floor for those who want it, and lively spaces for those who need to work together. The building has not lost its purpose, the report argues; it has gained several. A library that once stored knowledge now helps students to make it, argue with it and present it, which is a harder job and a more interesting one."},{"key":"2","text":"Reading has never been easier, yet many people say they read less than they used to. The explanation is not that books have become less interesting, but that attention has become harder to hold. A phone on the desk offers a constant supply of short messages, and each one gives the brain a small reward. In this environment, a novel that takes three pages to describe a single afternoon can feel slow, even boring.\n\nTeachers and researchers who study reading call the alternative deep reading. They mean the kind of reading in which a person follows an argument, notices the writer's tone and pauses over a sentence that seems especially well made. This does not happen automatically; it requires a quiet place, an unhurried mind and, above all, a decision to stay with one text.\n\nDigital devices are not the enemy. Many readers do their deep reading on tablets, and audiobooks have allowed busy people to return to books they would otherwise never open. What matters is whether the reading is planned or merely squeezed into gaps between messages.\n\nA growing number of universities have therefore introduced short courses on reading habits, in which students are asked to keep a record of when, where and how long they read. The results are often surprising. Students discover that thirty minutes of concentration in the morning is worth more than three hours of half-attention, and that the books they remember most are the ones they read slowly. Perhaps the lesson is simply that reading, like any other skill, improves when it is given time."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 02","total":22,"wordbank":["benefit","connect","effective","entirely","eventually","memory","obvious","planning","plot","record","replace","retain","substitute","tool","wisely"],"materials":{"cloze":"Watching films is one of the most enjoyable ways to learn a foreign language, but it works best when it is done with some (26) ____. Many learners simply switch on the subtitles in their own language and follow the story, which is entertaining but does little for their vocabulary. A better method is to watch a short scene twice: first with English subtitles and then without. On the second viewing, the brain is forced to (27) ____ the sound of words with their meaning. It also helps to (28) ____ difficult expressions in a notebook and to look them up afterwards. Research on vocabulary learning suggests that a word must be met many times before it is remembered, so choosing one film and watching it again after a week may be more (29) ____ than watching five new films in a row. Teachers warn, however, that films should not (30) ____ other kinds of practice. Speaking, reading and writing still need time. Used (31) ____, though, films can bring the language to life and remind learners why they are studying it. The (32) ____ of the story also gives the brain something to hold on to, which makes new words easier to (33) ____. In short, films are not a (34) ____ for hard work, but a (35) ____ that makes the work more pleasant.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Banks, bus companies and government offices have moved most of their services onto the internet, and the change has left many older people behind. A person who once paid every bill at the counter may now be asked to download an app, create a password and confirm a code sent by message. For those who have never owned a smartphone, these small tasks can feel like a locked door. The charity behind the sessions says that calls for help have doubled in three years, and most come from people over seventy."},{"key":"B","text":"The sessions take place on Tuesday and Friday mornings in a quiet room at the back of the town library. Volunteers put out long tables, a kettle and a box of written instructions in large print. Learners arrive with their own phones, often still in the box, and sit beside the volunteer they have been paired with. There is no lecture and no fixed programme; people bring the problems they met during the week, and the room slowly fills with quiet conversation."},{"key":"C","text":"The first lesson is always the same. Learners practise switching the phone on and off, making a call, sending a short message and adding a name to the contacts list. Only when these actions feel natural do they move on to photographs or the internet. Volunteers say it is a mistake to begin with the most exciting features; a person who cannot answer a call will soon lose confidence. Simple actions are repeated until they become habits, and each small success makes the next lesson easier."},{"key":"D","text":"Each learner works with the same student volunteer every week. This pairing matters more than it might seem. The volunteer learns what the person already knows, which buttons frighten them and how quickly they tire, and the learner does not have to explain the same difficulties to a stranger each time. One volunteer describes her role as a patient neighbour rather than a teacher. When her learner finally sent a photograph to her grandchild, she says, both of them were close to tears."},{"key":"E","text":"Not every danger comes from a broken phone. Volunteers spend a full session on fraud, because older people are often targeted by callers pretending to be from a bank. Learners are shown how to recognise a suspicious message, why they should never share a password or bank details, and what to do if money has already been taken. The advice is written on a card that fits in a purse. Several learners have since stopped a scam themselves, and one brought the card to the police station to show an officer."},{"key":"F","text":"The charity measures success in small events: a first video call with a grandchild, a bus ticket bought on the phone, a photograph sent to a friend. These moments rarely appear in national statistics, but they change a person's week. Volunteers also report a benefit they did not expect. Many learners stay after the session for tea and begin to help each other, so the room has become a social club as much as a classroom. The project now runs in eleven towns, and the waiting list grows every month."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Every new technology that enters education seems to attract the same argument. Supporters say it will help students who are struggling; critics say it will make them lazy. AI tutoring tools are no exception. These programs can answer questions in clear language, explain a grammar point for the tenth time without losing patience, and give a student immediate feedback at midnight. For learners in places where good tutors are expensive or far away, that is a serious advantage. Studies of basic mathematics and language practice suggest that students who use such tools regularly do improve their test scores, especially when the tool is used for short, focused sessions.\n\nThe doubts begin elsewhere. A machine can produce a confident answer that is simply wrong, and a student who has not learned to check may accept it. There is also a quieter worry: if a program always supplies the next step, students may never experience the struggle that teaches them how to think. Teachers report that some students now submit work that is grammatically flawless but strangely empty, as if no one had an opinion behind it.\n\nMost experts therefore draw a sensible line. AI can explain, test and remind; it cannot care whether the student understands. The human teacher remains the person who notices a silent pupil, changes the plan halfway through a lesson and asks the question that makes a class think. In that division of labour, the machine is useful only if the teacher is in charge."},{"key":"2","text":"Ten years ago, a student who wanted to study had to leave the internet behind. The phone stayed in a bag, the laptop was used for one task at a time, and a long evening of reading was normal. Today the same student may have six tabs open, a group chat buzzing and a video playing in the corner of the screen.\n\nThe result is not that young people cannot concentrate, but that they concentrate in shorter bursts. Researchers who watch students studying describe a pattern of rapid switching: a few minutes on the essay, a quick check of messages, back to the essay, then a search for a fact that turns into twenty minutes of reading something unrelated. Each switch feels harmless, yet the cost adds up.\n\nAfter an interruption, the mind needs time to return to the point where it stopped, and some studies estimate that this recovery takes several minutes.\n\nThe good news is that attention can be trained. Experiments in which students put their phones in another room and worked for fixed periods found that they completed tasks faster and remembered more. Many now use simple methods: a timer for twenty-five minutes of work, a notebook for distracting thoughts, and one evening a week without a screen. None of these ideas is new. What is new is that they have to be taught, because the digital world will not switch itself off."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 03","total":22,"wordbank":["different","durable","effective","enough","entire","extend","obvious","power","rarely","reduce","surely","tackle","thrown","wisely","collect"],"materials":{"cloze":"Every year the world produces far more clothes than people can wear. Prices are low, designs change quickly, and a T-shirt may be worn a few times before it is (26) ____ away. The results are easy to see: mountains of clothing in landfills and rivers polluted by the dyes used in factories. Some companies have begun to (27) ____ the problem by collecting old clothes in their shops, but only a small share of what is collected is turned into new fabric. Most is sold to other countries or burned. Consumers have more (28) ____ than they realise. Washing clothes at a lower temperature and drying them in the air can (29) ____ their life by years, and simple repairs can save a favourite jacket from the bin. Buying (30) ____, well-made clothes costs more at first but saves money later. Campaigners also encourage people to organise swap events, where friends bring clothes they no longer wear and take home something (31) ____. None of these actions is (32) ____ on its own, but together they (33) ____ the pressure on the planet. The most (34) ____ change, campaigners argue, is to buy less and choose (35) ____.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"A green roof is exactly what its name suggests: a building whose flat top has been covered with soil and plants. Under the visible layer there are several others that most people never see — a waterproof membrane, a drainage system and a root barrier. The weight has to be calculated before anything is planted, and older buildings sometimes need extra support. Once the layers are in place, the roof can be planted with grass, moss, wild flowers or small shrubs, depending on how much weight the structure can carry."},{"key":"B","text":"Cities are usually several degrees warmer than the countryside around them, because concrete and asphalt absorb heat during the day and release it slowly at night. Plants work in the opposite way. They take up water and release it as vapour, a process that cools the air, and the soil beneath them insulates the building, so less energy is needed for air conditioning. Measurements on a green roof in summer have found the surface temperature to be up to thirty degrees lower than that of a conventional roof next door."},{"key":"C","text":"When a heavy storm arrives, drains in a modern city can be overwhelmed within minutes. A green roof slows the water down. Rain is caught by the plants and soil, and what does not evaporate or is taken up by the roots runs off more slowly, sometimes hours after the rain has stopped. In cities that have suffered from flooding, this delay is valuable. Green roofs cannot replace proper drainage, engineers warn, but they can reduce the peak load on a system during the worst hour of a storm."},{"key":"D","text":"A city centre is a difficult place for a bird, a bee or a butterfly. Green roofs create small islands of habitat above the traffic, and research in several European cities has recorded bees, beetles and nesting birds on them. The value depends on the plants chosen: a roof covered with a single type of moss supports far less life than one planted with a mixture of flowers and grasses. Connecting such roofs across a district, rather than building one isolated example, makes a real difference to insects that need stepping stones between green spaces."},{"key":"E","text":"The obvious objection is cost. Installing a green roof can cost two or three times as much as a standard one, and the building owner may not stay long enough to see the savings. Supporters reply that the calculation is wider than the roof itself. The roof lasts longer because the membrane is protected from sunlight, the building uses less energy for cooling, and the city saves money on drainage. Some cities now offer grants or allow developers to build higher when they include a green roof, which changes the sums for everyone involved."},{"key":"F","text":"Green roofs are no longer an experiment. In Singapore, new buildings are required to include planting on their roofs, and the city's most famous hotel has a garden that runs along its top like a park. In Germany, the idea has been common for decades, and several cities offer financial support. Closer to home, schools and offices have used their flat roofs for gardens, vegetable plots and even small sports areas. Supporters say the examples are spreading because the benefits are easy to explain, and a visitor can see them from street level."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Universities are often described as small cities, and like cities they consume a great deal of energy, produce a great deal of waste and own a great many buildings. It is not surprising, therefore, that a growing number of campuses have started to treat sustainability as a practical problem rather than a subject for discussion. The measures they take are rarely dramatic. One university replaced the lights in its library with sensors that switch off when nobody is in the room, which cut electricity use in the building by a fifth. Another began serving smaller portions in its dining halls and charging less for them, and found that food waste fell by almost a third in a year. Some have built cycle lanes between halls and lecture rooms or offered free bus passes to students who choose not to bring a car.\n\nWhat interests researchers most is not the technology but the way these changes are introduced. Projects that begin with a student society tend to last longer than those announced by the administration, because students keep using them after the official launch. A survey of twelve campuses found that the most successful projects were small, visible and easy to join: a repair workshop, a book swap, a garden behind the science building. Big targets may look impressive in a report, but a student is more likely to change one habit than to rethink an entire lifestyle. That, the researchers conclude, is how change actually starts."},{"key":"2","text":"Bike-sharing schemes were once presented as the answer to almost every urban problem. They would reduce traffic, cut pollution, improve public health and fill the gaps between bus stops and homes. In many cities the first months were indeed a success: brightly coloured bicycles appeared on every corner, and people who had not ridden since childhood began cycling to work.\n\nThen came the problems. Bicycles were left in the middle of pavements, thrown into rivers and piled outside railway stations. Companies competing for the same streets put more bicycles on the road than the city could manage. Several councils responded by limiting the number of bikes and requiring operators to remove those that were badly parked. The result has been a quieter, more useful service. Where parking spaces are clearly marked and repairs are quick, shared bikes have become a normal part of city life. Where they are not, the bicycles soon become rubbish.\n\nThe lesson seems to be that a vehicle shared by thousands of people needs the same care as a public library: someone must check it, clean it and put it back in the right place. Technology can help, but the bicycle on the pavement is still a human problem."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 04","total":22,"wordbank":["affordable","argue","balance","claimed","creative","essential","forgotten","genuine","gradually","handled","likely","regular","steady","supply","transport"],"materials":{"cloze":"Every city has buildings that tell its story, yet many of them disappear before residents have time to think. In the past, planners often (26) ____ that old structures were simply in the way of progress. Today a different view is spreading, and heritage protection has become an (27) ____ part of urban planning.\n\nRepair is rarely cheap. A stone arch or a wooden roof may need months of work by specialists whose skills are in short (28) ____. Supporters say repair costs far less than loss. Once a building is gone, the stories attached to it are (29) ____ as well.\n\nSome cities have found (30) ____ solutions. In one old district, a former textile factory became a library. Tourists come, but the (31) ____ visitors are local people who use the reading rooms every day. The project also provided (32) ____ work for craftsmen who might otherwise have left the area.\n\nCritics still (33) ____ that such projects mainly attract wealthy newcomers and push poorer residents out. That risk is real, and it must be (34) ____ seriously. But the answer is not to demolish everything; it is to plan carefully and accept that a city without memory is (35) ____ to lose its identity.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"For most of the twentieth century a museum was a quiet hall of glass cases, and the visitor's job was to look. Curators now describe their work differently: they want people to leave with a story rather than a list of dates. Labels have grown longer, and rooms are arranged around questions instead of centuries. In one city museum, the first object a visitor meets is not a crown but a schoolboy's letter written in 1916."},{"key":"B","text":"Families were once tolerated rather than welcomed. Today many museums run special mornings when the lights are brighter, the paths are shorter and touching is allowed. Staff are trained to kneel down when they speak to a six-year-old. The change is not only generous; it is practical. Children who enjoy a visit bring their parents back, and school groups have become one of the most reliable sources of income for smaller museums."},{"key":"C","text":"Museums are also opening their back doors. Several now rent desk space to researchers, run evening language classes and allow students to use their libraries after dark. A museum in Manchester offers a quiet study room to local teenagers during the exam season. Its director argues that a building which belongs to the public should be useful every day, not only on the two afternoons a week when most tourists appear."},{"key":"D","text":"None of this is easy to pay for. Government grants have fallen in many countries, and the cost of heating an old stone building keeps rising. Some museums have introduced ticket prices, a decision that always causes argument. Others lend objects to commercial exhibitions abroad. Most directors agree that a museum cannot survive on charm alone, and that a clear financial plan matters as much as a good exhibition."},{"key":"E","text":"In the past, decisions were made by experts behind closed doors. Some museums now invite residents to choose what should be displayed. In one town, a public meeting decided that the museum's largest room should be given to the history of local factories rather than to a collection of coins. Attendance doubled within a year. Visitors say they come because the place finally speaks about their own streets and families."},{"key":"F","text":"The danger of chasing visitors is that a museum may lose the very thing that makes it valuable. If every room must be interactive and every label amusing, objects become background. Critics point out that serious research and slow-looking exhibitions are expensive and rarely attract headlines. The best institutions, they argue, keep a balance: a noisy family morning on Saturday does not mean that Tuesday afternoon has to be full of screens."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"In 2019, a fire broke out in the roof of Notre-Dame in Paris. Within hours, engineers were able to consult a detailed three-dimensional model of the cathedral that had been built a few years earlier by laser scanning. Every stone, every arch and every piece of timber had been recorded to within a few millimetres. The model did not prevent the fire, but it made the rebuilding far more accurate than anyone would have thought possible.\n\nThe example has encouraged museums and heritage sites around the world to digitise their collections. Some use drones to photograph buildings from above, while others carry portable scanners into caves or narrow temples. At Lascaux, researchers have spent decades recording the prehistoric cave paintings at very high resolution, and the images are now used for both research and education.\n\nDigital copies are not only a backup. They allow people to see objects that are too fragile to be displayed. A page from a tenth-century manuscript may be so sensitive to light that it can be shown to the public only once every few years, yet its digital image can be studied by scholars in Beijing, London or Cairo on the same afternoon. Visitors to a museum in a small town can walk through a reconstruction of a palace destroyed six hundred years ago.\n\nCritics warn that a digital copy is not the building itself. Screens may also tempt governments to pay for images rather than for repairs, since a scan is cheaper than a new roof. Supporters accept that risk but reply that recording a site is the first step towards saving it. 'A building that has been properly recorded,' one architect says, 'is a building we can argue for with evidence.'"},{"key":"2","text":"When Zhou Lan told her parents that she wanted to become a book restorer, they imagined a quiet job in a library. In reality she spends her days in a workshop in Nanjing, bent over a fragile volume with a fine brush, wheat paste and paper of a dozen different weights. One torn sheet may take three hours to mend, and a badly damaged book can keep her busy for a month.\n\nTraditional book repair was once a small trade, passed from master to apprentice inside a few families. For years, young people showed little interest: the pay was low, the work was slow, and success was invisible. The turning point came when a documentary about restorers became unexpectedly popular online. Applications to the national training programme rose from a handful to several hundred, and the average age of the students fell from forty to twenty-six.\n\nZhou Lan says the appeal is partly the silence. 'Everything around us moves fast,' she explains. 'Here, if you hurry, you tear the paper.' She also enjoys the moment when an object returns to use. Recently she spent five weeks repairing an eighteenth-century dictionary that a village school had owned for two hundred years, and she refused to accept payment from the school.\n\nNot everyone is comfortable with the attention. Some older craftspeople complain that television crews now visit the workshop in search of beautiful pictures rather than asking about technique, and that a short film cannot show the ten years needed to train an eye. Zhou Lan understands the complaint, but she thinks the interest is a beginning. 'People come for the pictures,' she says. 'Some of them stay for the paper.'"}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 05","total":22,"wordbank":["awkward","consistency","formed","give","include","learn","matters","offer","recognise","reliable","seriously","take","trust","usual","weekly"],"materials":{"cloze":"Volunteering is usually described as a gift of time, but its benefits do not flow in one direction only. Students who spend a few hours a week at a community centre often say they gain as much as they (26) ____. The first weeks, though, can feel (27) ____: a new place, unfamiliar faces and tasks that look simple but are not.\n\nWhat makes the difference is (28) ____ — turning up every week rather than once, when nothing else is planned. Organisers say the most (29) ____ volunteers are not always the most talented ones, but those who keep their promises.\n\nVolunteering also teaches skills that are hard to (30) ____ in a classroom, such as listening patiently and explaining an idea in simple words. Employers have begun to (31) ____ such experience, and some universities now (32) ____ volunteer work in the record students take to interviews.\n\nThere are limits, of course. Volunteering should not (33) ____ the place of paid work, nor should it damage a student's studies. What (34) ____ is a modest but steady commitment, and the habit, once (35) ____, often lasts for life.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"The story began with a piece of land behind a bus stop, covered in weeds and broken bottles. In 2016 a group of residents asked the city council whether they could grow vegetables there. The answer was a one-year trial. They cleared the ground with borrowed tools, built twelve raised beds and carried the soil in wheelbarrows. Nothing about the site was beautiful, yet by the end of the first summer strangers were stopping to look over the fence and to ask whether they could join the waiting list."},{"key":"B","text":"A garden with forty members needs a system. Each household looks after one bed and one shared job: the compost, the water pipes, the tools or the notice board. Two people open the gate on weekday mornings, and a rota covers the weekends. Nobody is paid, and nobody is allowed to do everything. Members say the rota matters more than the rules on paper, because it shows new members how much work one tomato plant demands."},{"key":"C","text":"Nearby primary schools treat the garden as an open-air classroom. Classes come in spring and autumn to plant beans, measure growth and draw what they see. One teacher uses the garden to teach science, another to discuss healthy eating. Children who refuse vegetables at school lunch often eat them raw here. For many of them this is the first time they have seen food growing in soil rather than arriving in a plastic bag on a supermarket shelf."},{"key":"D","text":"Harvest day falls on the last Saturday of the month. Members keep what they need, and the rest goes to a small table by the gate with a sign saying 'help yourself'. Some of it reaches elderly neighbours who can no longer shop easily, and some goes to a food bank two streets away. The gardeners do not regard this as charity; they simply believe that a place which grows food should also feed the street around it."},{"key":"E","text":"No garden is peaceful all the time. Arguments have been about water bills, about loud music and about a plum tree that shaded someone's tomatoes. The committee's answer is to meet for twenty minutes on harvest day, with a rule that nobody may discuss a problem at the gate. Most disagreements are settled there; when one is not, the member who feels unheard may bring it to the council officer who supervises the site."},{"key":"F","text":"The one-year trial ended long ago, but the land remains uncertain. The council could sell the site for housing, and the gardeners know that a good season proves nothing about the lease. In 2022 they helped to write a neighbourhood plan which keeps the garden in public use for fifteen years. Meanwhile they plant fruit trees, which will take years to fruit. 'You do not plant a tree for yourself,' one member says. 'You plant it for whoever comes next.'"}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"When universities advertise volunteering, they usually stress what students can do for the community. A growing number of careers advisers are more interested in the opposite question: what the community does for students.\n\nThe evidence suggests that this second effect is larger than students expect. A study following two hundred undergraduates over a year found that those who volunteered for two hours a week were more likely to say they could cope with difficult people and unexpected problems. The improvement did not depend on the sort of work they did. Sorting clothes in a charity shop and teaching children to swim produced similar changes, possibly because both require turning up, paying attention and being useful to someone who does not care about your grades.\n\nEmployers seem to notice. In a survey of graduate recruiters, more than half said that volunteering was useful evidence of qualities that interviews cannot easily reveal, such as reliability. One recruiter admitted that a student who had run a weekend football club for three years told her more about commitment than any number of prizes.\n\nThere is a risk of exaggeration. Students who volunteer mainly to fill a line on their CV often stop after a few weeks, and a single afternoon of painting a wall proves very little. Advisers therefore suggest choosing one organisation and staying with it for a year. They also warn against letting hours push aside sleep, study or paid work, since a student who is exhausted helps nobody.\n\nThe most lasting benefit may be quieter than any of these. Volunteers meet people whose lives they would otherwise never enter, and they learn that a community is not managed by officials alone. That lesson is hard to test, which may be why it is so rarely advertised."},{"key":"2","text":"In many cities, the people who live closest together know least about each other. A recent experiment in three neighbourhoods tried to change that with a very old idea: a list of what neighbours can offer one another.\n\nThe project began with a simple questionnaire delivered to eight hundred homes. Residents were asked to write down one skill they would be willing to share, such as repairing a bicycle or filling in a form, and one thing they needed help with. The replies were surprising in two ways. Only about a fifth of the forms came back, yet those who replied offered far more than the organisers had expected. More importantly, the most frequent request was not practical at all: many people asked for company, someone to walk with in the evenings, or a neighbour willing to knock on the door once a week.\n\nThe organisers resisted the temptation to build an app. They printed a small booklet instead and asked the manager of the local shop to keep copies behind the counter. Older residents told them that a screen was exactly the thing that had made them feel excluded. A booklet, by contrast, could be read at the bus stop and passed on to a friend.\n\nTwo years later the project survives in a modest form: a monthly repair afternoon, a walking group on Tuesdays, and a list of people who will lend a ladder or a drill. The organisers measure success not by the number of services exchanged but by how quickly newcomers are noticed. 'In a good network,' one of them says, 'a new family is invited to something within a month, without anyone being officially asked to do it.'"}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 06","total":22,"wordbank":["acceptable","attention","confuse","easy","evidence","mistakes","naturally","offence","overnight","polite","rubbing","simplest","skill","uncomfortable","warning"],"materials":{"cloze":"Researchers who study body language like to remind us that words carry only part of any message. The rest comes from the face, the hands and the distance we keep from other people. A gesture that is friendly in one country can cause (26) ____ in another; a distance that feels natural may make another person (27) ____.\n\nThis does not mean that body language is a list of rules to be learned (28) ____. Most people manage it unconsciously. If a visitor keeps (29) ____ his hands and shifting his feet, we may decide, without any (30) ____, that he is nervous or dishonest, when he is simply cold or tired.\n\nThe (31) ____ advice is therefore to slow down. Watch how people sit and how long they look at each other. Ask a colleague whether a habit that seems natural to you is (32) ____ in the new setting. Above all, do not (33) ____ politeness with agreement: a person may be extremely polite and still firmly refuse your idea.\n\nNone of this is (34) ____. Reading the wrong signal is a mistake everyone makes, and the ability to laugh at one's own (35) ____ is itself a form of cultural skill.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"It started with two neighbours and a borrowed table. In 2015, a retired teacher and a restaurant owner in the Riverside district decided that the many foreign families in their street should have a chance to meet the older residents. They set up one table outside the community centre with dumplings on one side and bread on the other. About thirty people came. The following year the district office offered a small square, and the festival was born."},{"key":"B","text":"Food remains the centre of the event. Last year forty cooking stalls stretched along the river, and the queue for a Vietnamese soup was longer than the queue for anything else. Cooks are encouraged to stand beside their dishes and explain what is in them, so that visitors leave with a recipe rather than only a memory. Every stall must offer at least one small portion for a token price, so that a family with children can try many things."},{"key":"C","text":"The stage is deliberately mixed. A professional folk group from the local theatre performs in the afternoon, but the evening programme belongs to amateurs: a primary school choir, a group of students learning flamenco, and a band of neighbours who met at the festival two years ago. Auditions are open to anyone, and the only rule is that each act must last no more than eight minutes, since a tired audience stops listening and starts queuing for food."},{"key":"D","text":"Behind the scenes, about sixty volunteers keep the festival running. They guide visitors who cannot find a stall, hand out programmes at the bridge, help elderly residents find seats and collect rubbish at the end of the night. New volunteers attend a short briefing a week in advance, and each is paired with someone who worked the previous year. Organisers say the volunteers, not the stage, decide whether the festival feels friendly."},{"key":"E","text":"Money is the permanent worry. The district provides the square and pays for electricity, but everything else must be found. Two years ago a sudden storm destroyed half the tents, and the festival ended with a debt. Since then the organisers have asked local businesses for support, in exchange for a line on the programme. A cloth bag with the festival logo also raises a modest but reliable sum each year."},{"key":"F","text":"Success has brought a new problem: size. Some residents now complain about noise, traffic and litter, and the district has asked the organisers to keep the event inside the square. Others would like to see it grow into a three-day fair that attracts visitors from other cities. The committee has postponed any decision for a year and, in the meantime, has begun to count how many people arrive by bicycle."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"When students go abroad, they usually prepare for language problems. Few prepare for the quieter difficulty of everyday habits.\n\nA month after arriving in Germany, a Chinese student found that she was exhausted every evening, although her classes were no longer than they had been at home. Nothing was wrong with her health. Her energy was going into small decisions: whether to greet a neighbour in the corridor, how long to wait for a bus that might not come, whether the silence of a shared kitchen meant that her flatmates disliked her. Each choice took seconds, but there were hundreds of them a day.\n\nResearchers who study adaptation call this 'cultural fatigue'. It is not a sign of weakness, and it does not mean that the move was a mistake. It appears in students who are doing well academically as well as in those who are homesick. The usual advice, such as making local friends and joining clubs, is sound but incomplete, because it describes the cure without naming the illness.\n\nWhat seems to help most is routine. Students who keep one or two fixed habits, such as a Saturday morning run or a weekly cooking evening with the same people, report feeling more settled than those who try to be social every night. Predictability saves energy, and energy is exactly what adaptation demands.\n\nThe second helpful thing is a sense of humour about mistakes. Students who can laugh when they use the wrong word in a shop recover faster than those who treat every misunderstanding as a personal failure. Nobody speaks a foreign language perfectly in the first month, and the people serving you are usually too busy to remember your mistake five minutes later."},{"key":"2","text":"For many years, language teaching in schools concentrated on grammar and vocabulary, and a student could pass an examination without ever hearing a real conversation. That has changed, but not everywhere, and the results of the change are worth examining.\n\nConsider the word 'sorry'. A dictionary will tell you that it expresses regret. In practice, a speaker of English may use it to apologise, to ask someone to repeat a sentence, to interrupt a meeting, or to express sympathy. A learner who knows only the dictionary meaning will sound either too formal or strangely cold. Researchers call this kind of knowledge 'pragmatic competence', and they find that it is often missing in students with high test scores.\n\nThe good news is that it can be taught. In one experiment, two classes of students at the same level were compared. Both studied the same vocabulary, but one class also watched short recordings of everyday situations and discussed why the speaker chose a particular phrase. After twelve weeks, that class performed much better in a role-play task, even though the grammar test results of the two groups were almost identical.\n\nNone of this argues against grammar. Accuracy matters, especially in writing. The argument is about balance: a language is not only a system of rules but also a set of habits used by a community. Learning a language without its culture is like reading a menu without knowing what the dishes taste like."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 07","total":22,"wordbank":["achieve","argue","conclusive","delay","establish","ignore","indicate","motivated","obstacle","permanent","recommend","reduce","regret","roughly","suggests"],"materials":{"cloze":"短文（空白处标有 26–35）：\n\nLearning a Musical Instrument in Adulthood\n\nMany adults believe that learning to play a musical instrument is a task for children, yet a growing body of research (26) ____ that the adult brain is far more adaptable than was once thought. Teachers of adult beginners report that their students are often more (27) ____ than teenagers, because they choose to be there.\n\nThe main (28) ____ for adults is time. Work and family duties leave little space for daily practice, and progress can seem painfully slow. Teachers therefore (29) ____ short, frequent sessions rather than one long one at the weekend. Fifteen minutes a day is enough to (30) ____ a new habit.\n\nAdults also tend to be hard on themselves. A wrong note that a child barely notices may make an adult want to give up entirely. Experienced teachers (31) ____ this anxiety by choosing simple pieces at first and by celebrating small improvements.\n\nThe benefits go beyond music. Studies (32) ____ that playing an instrument engages memory, attention and coordination at the same time. Some researchers (33) ____ that this mental exercise may help keep the brain healthy in later life, although the evidence is not yet (34) ____.\n\nWhat is clear is that adults who begin lessons rarely (35) ____ the decision, even if they never perform in public.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Growing demand\nFarmers' markets were once a small weekend activity in a few towns. Over the past decade their number has grown steadily, and many now run twice a week. Part of the reason is that shoppers ask more questions about food: where it was grown, when it was picked and how it was transported. Stallholders can answer these questions directly, which supermarkets, with their long supply chains, find much harder to do."},{"key":"B","text":"A shorter journey\nFood sold at a market usually travels only a few miles from field to stall. That means it can be picked closer to ripeness, and it does not need the cold storage that long-distance transport requires. Several studies suggest that local produce can also lose fewer nutrients. The saving in transport is real, though researchers caution that small deliveries made by many individual vans may not always be greener overall."},{"key":"C","text":"Bringing people together\nA market is more than a place to buy vegetables. Regular customers learn the names of stallholders, and neighbours meet while queuing for bread. In some towns the market has become the centre of community life, hosting cooking demonstrations and children's activities. Shopkeepers nearby report that the visitors also spend money in their businesses, so the benefit spreads beyond the stalls themselves."},{"key":"D","text":"Challenges for small farmers\nRunning a stall is hard work. Farmers must leave home before dawn, pay for the pitch and still look after their land. Bad weather can ruin a whole day's income. Some smaller producers have joined together to share a stall and transport costs. Without such cooperation, many would find that selling directly takes more time than they can spare from actual farming."},{"key":"E","text":"Changing habits of young people\nMarket organisers say the average customer is getting younger. Students and young professionals come for a cheap lunch, then leave with unfamiliar vegetables they have never cooked before. Online videos have helped: a short clip showing how to prepare a strange-looking root can turn it into a bestseller the following weekend. This experimentation is slowly changing the way young people plan their meals."},{"key":"F","text":"What the future may hold\nMost people involved in farmers' markets are optimistic. As food prices rise and concern about the environment grows, buying directly from producers looks attractive. Permanent indoor market halls, card payments and home delivery are already appearing in some cities. Observers warn, however, that rising rents may push markets away from the very neighbourhoods that need them most."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Every spring, thousands of students compete for summer internships, and the pressure is easy to understand. A recent survey of graduates found that those who had completed at least one internship received more job offers than those who had none. But the same survey produced a more interesting result: the length of the internship mattered less than the quality of the feedback the students received.\n\nThis finding challenges a common assumption. Many students believe that simply adding a famous company's name to their CV is enough. In practice, interviewers often ask what the candidate actually did. A student who spent three months photocopying documents in a large firm may have less to say than one who spent six weeks in a small studio where the manager explained every decision.\n\nThe nature of the work matters too. Tasks that require judgement, such as writing a short report, contacting clients or solving a practical problem, teach skills that cannot be learned from a textbook. By contrast, repetitive tasks may look impressive on paper but leave little to discuss in an interview.\n\nThere is also the question of payment. Unpaid internships are often criticised because they favour students whose families can support them. Some employers have responded by offering a modest allowance and travel costs, and a few universities now fund summer placements for students from lower-income backgrounds.\n\nThe lesson for students is clear. Before accepting an internship, ask what you will be doing, who will supervise you and whether anyone will comment on your work. A famous logo may open a door, but it is the experience behind the door that employers actually value."},{"key":"2","text":"Li Na delivers food on an electric bike in a large city in eastern China. She chooses her own hours, works for three different platforms, and earns roughly what she did in her previous office job. I do not have a manager watching me, she says. But if I am ill, nobody pays me, and if the app changes its rules, I cannot argue.\n\nLi Na is one of millions of young people in the growing gig economy, where work is organised through apps and paid by the task rather than by the month. Supporters describe it as freedom: students can earn between lectures, parents can work around childcare, and retired people can take on a few hours a week. For people who find fixed schedules difficult, this flexibility is genuinely valuable.\n\nThe disadvantages, however, are also real. Gig workers usually have no paid holiday, no sick leave and no pension contributions from an employer. Their income changes with demand, weather and the decisions of the platforms. When a company alters its payment rules overnight, workers have little power to negotiate, because they are not employees in the legal sense.\n\nSome governments are now experimenting with a middle category that would give gig workers basic protections while keeping their flexibility. Others are leaving the question to the platforms. Either way, the debate is no longer about whether the gig economy should exist, but about how the people in it should be treated.\n\nYoung people entering this world need more than a smartphone. They need to understand how their pay is calculated, keep records of their earnings, and think about what happens when they are forty rather than twenty-five. Flexibility is a benefit only if it is chosen with open eyes."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 08","total":22,"wordbank":["attract","avoid","budget","decline","frequently","freedom","generous","harmless","maintain","precise","refer","rule","similar","warn","wording"],"materials":{"cloze":"短文（空白处标有 26–35）：\n\nReading Food Labels\n\nWhen you pick up a packet in a supermarket, the front is designed to (26) ____ your attention. Words such as 'natural' or 'light' are chosen to sound healthy, but they have no (27) ____ legal meaning in many countries. The information that really matters is printed on the back, often in very small type.\n\nLearning to read a label takes only a few minutes. First, check the size of the portion the numbers (28) ____ to. A bag that seems to contain 200 calories may in fact hold three portions. Second, compare the same kind of product rather than two different foods, because (29) ____ values are easier to judge.\n\nNutritionists advise shoppers to (30) ____ products with a long list of unfamiliar ingredients. Usually a short list means the food has been processed less. They also (31) ____ that sugar appears under many names, so a product may contain three kinds of sweetener and still look (32) ____ at first glance.\n\nFood companies argue that labels give consumers (33) ____ to make their own choices. Campaigners agree in principle but want the (34) ____ to be clearer. Until then, shoppers can protect themselves with one simple (35) ____: turn the packet over and read the small print before you decide.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Time away from screens\nParents who are tired of arguing about screen time have found a simple alternative: a box of cards or a board. A recent survey of 2,000 families found that four in ten now play games together at least once a week, and most of them say the main reason is to spend an evening without phones. Teachers report similar experiments in classrooms, where short games are used to warm up a lesson."},{"key":"B","text":"Old games, new players\nGames that grandparents played are returning to the shelves. Shops report strong demand for new editions of classic word and card games, often bought by people in their twenties and thirties. Designers have improved the artwork and shortened the rules, so a game that once took an hour to explain can now be taught in five minutes. Social media has helped, as players share photographs of their evenings online."},{"key":"C","text":"A boost for small shops\nIn many cities, board game cafes have opened in the past few years. Customers pay a small fee, borrow games from the shelves and order drinks while they play. For the owners of small shops, this is a way to compete with online retailers: what is being sold is not the box but an evening out. Some cafes also run tournaments that fill quiet weekday evenings."},{"key":"D","text":"Learning to lose\nTeachers and psychologists point to benefits that have little to do with the games themselves. Waiting for your turn, explaining a plan, disagreeing without losing your temper and accepting defeat are all practised at the table. One primary school now holds a weekly game afternoon and says arguments in the playground have become less frequent, because pupils have learned to separate the result of a game from the person who won it."},{"key":"E","text":"Barriers to growth\nNot everything is easy. Translating and printing a game for a new market can cost far more than the designers expect, and small publishers struggle to compete with large companies. Illegal copies appear quickly online. Designers also complain that a successful idea is imitated within months, and that the law offers little protection to a set of rules or a piece of cardboard."},{"key":"F","text":"The table as a meeting place\nFor adults who move to a new city, a games evening can be an unusually easy way to meet people. Unlike a party, it gives strangers something to do and a reason to talk. Organisers of public game nights say that regulars often arrive alone and leave in groups, and that the atmosphere is friendlier than in a bar, because everyone is concentrating on the same table."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Students often treat sleep as the first thing to cut when exams approach. A week of late nights seems a small price to pay for extra revision. Research on memory, however, suggests that this bargain is a poor one: the hours lost at night cannot simply be replaced by hours spent with a textbook.\n\nThe reason lies in what happens after learning. When we first take in new information, the memory is fragile. During sleep, and especially during deep sleep in the first half of the night, the brain replays and reorganises what it has taken in, linking new material to what we already know. Cut that sleep short and the links are weaker. In one experiment, students who learned a list of words and then slept for eight hours remembered far more the next morning than those who stayed awake and revised for the same length of time.\n\nThere is a second effect, less obvious but just as important. Tired students find it harder to concentrate and are more easily distracted by their phones. They may sit at a desk for three hours and still absorb very little. Their teachers notice the difference before they do: the same student who was quick and curious in October becomes slow and quiet in December.\n\nThe practical advice from researchers is unglamorous. Keep a regular bedtime, avoid caffeine after the early afternoon, and put screens away before going to bed. If a deadline forces a late night, try to recover with a short nap the next day rather than with more coffee. Sleep, they argue, is not time taken away from study. It is part of study."},{"key":"2","text":"Every January, gyms fill with new members, and by March many of them have quietly disappeared. The pattern is so familiar that fitness staff call it the resolution curve. But a small group of researchers who study habit formation believe the problem is not motivation. It is the way people design their plans.\n\nIn one study, participants were asked to exercise three times a week for twelve weeks. Half of them were told to choose any time and place that suited them; the other half were asked to link the exercise to an existing daily routine, such as walking straight to the gym after a morning lecture. The second group attended about twice as many sessions. The explanation is simple: a new behaviour needs a reliable signal, and an existing routine provides one.\n\nThe researchers also found that starting very small helped. People who began with ten minutes on a bicycle were more likely to still be exercising after a month than those who began with an hour. Ten minutes feels too easy to skip, and once the habit exists it can be lengthened. A failed attempt at an ambitious plan, by contrast, often ends all attempts.\n\nSocial surroundings matter as well. Students who exercised with a friend attended more regularly, and those who told their roommates about their plan were less likely to give up. None of this requires money or special equipment. It requires a fixed moment, a small first step and someone who notices whether you turn up. That is probably why the oldest advice remains the best: put your sports shoes where you will see them in the morning."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 09","total":22,"wordbank":["advantage","composed","devote","doubt","familiar","linked","maintain","offer","recall","remote","standard","summarise","unfamiliar","valuable","warning"],"materials":{"cloze":"短文（空白处标有 26–35）：\n\nIs Handwriting Disappearing?\n\nTyping has become the (26) ____ way for students to take notes, and many of them can barely read their own handwriting. Teachers report that most essays are now (27) ____ on a screen, and that a page of cursive writing looks almost (28) ____ to some teenagers.\n\nYet handwriting may still have something to (29) ____. Researchers who compare typing with writing by hand find a small but consistent (30) ____: students who write notes by hand tend to remember more, probably because they must (31) ____ information and choose what matters, rather than copy every word.\n\nHandwriting is also (32) ____ to memory in another way. The physical movement of the pen leaves a trace that the brain can later (33) ____. For young children, forming letters by hand seems to help them recognise words.\n\nFew people (34) ____ that keyboards are here to stay. The question is whether schools should (35) ____ a little time each week to the old skill. Doing so is cheap, and it may give students something that a screen cannot.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Slower journeys, richer experiences\nA journey that takes ten hours is usually seen as a problem. Supporters of slow travel see it differently: the hours are part of the holiday. From a train window you notice villages, rivers and fields that disappear when you fly. You can also stop halfway, spend a night in a town you had never heard of, and continue the next morning. Many travellers say the unplanned stops are what they remember longest."},{"key":"B","text":"Night trains return to the timetable\nAfter years of decline, overnight trains are appearing again on European timetables. New routes link cities such as Vienna, Paris and Stockholm, and several more are planned. The appeal is obvious: you sleep while you move, and you arrive in the centre of a city rather than at an airport far from it. Tickets are not always cheap, however, and popular cabins sell out months in advance."},{"key":"C","text":"The cost of going slowly\nSlow travel has a price. A train ticket booked late can cost more than a budget flight, and every extra night in a hotel adds to the total. People with limited holidays may also find that two days of travelling leaves less time for the destination. For families, the cheaper option is often still the car, especially when luggage and children are taken into account. The choice, travellers admit, is rarely simple."},{"key":"D","text":"A smaller footprint\nTransport is a major source of greenhouse gases, and aviation is one of the fastest-growing parts of it. Measured per passenger, a train journey within Europe typically produces a small fraction of the carbon dioxide of the same trip by plane. This is the strongest argument for slow travel, and it is the one that governments mention most often when they promote rail. Yet the total number of flights continues to rise."},{"key":"E","text":"Business travellers stay away\nFor all the enthusiasm, slow travel remains a leisure activity. Companies rarely pay for an employee to spend two days on a train when a flight takes two hours, and video meetings have removed many short trips altogether. Some employers now allow extra travel days as part of a green policy, but such schemes are still unusual. Until business travel changes, the market for slow journeys will stay small."},{"key":"F","text":"Planning a route\nSlow travel rewards preparation more than fast travel does. Timetables must be checked, because a missed connection can mean a whole day lost. It helps to book seats on busy services and to keep the number of changes low. Experienced travellers also build in spare time, avoid the last train of the day and carry fewer bags. The aim is not a perfect plan but one that can survive a delay."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"For most of the last century, city planners measured success by how quickly cars could move. Wider roads, longer crossings and more parking spaces were seen as progress. In the past two decades, however, a different measure has become popular: how many people walk.\n\nThe change began with public health. Doctors pointed out that sitting in a car for an hour a day contributes to obesity and heart disease, while walking for thirty minutes brings clear benefits. Cities that made walking easier soon noticed something else: shops on busy pedestrian streets sold more, because walkers stop and look, while drivers pass by. Property values along attractive walking routes rose as well.\n\nSafety is the other half of the argument. In countries that redesigned streets to slow traffic, the number of serious accidents fell sharply. The most effective measures were simple and cheap: narrower lanes, raised crossings, more street trees and lower speed limits in residential areas. None of them required a new underground railway or a decade of construction.\n\nThere are limits. Pedestrian-friendly schemes can raise rents and push out the small businesses they were meant to help. People who live far from the centre may have no choice but to drive. And in very hot or very cold climates, walking is pleasant for only part of the year.\n\nEven so, the direction of travel has changed. City governments increasingly publish plans in which the first question is not how to move cars faster, but how to make the first ten minutes of a journey on foot something people are willing to do."},{"key":"2","text":"When a city builds a new square, it usually publishes photographs of clean paving, new benches and young trees. What it cannot photograph is whether anyone will use the space. Planners have learned, often painfully, that an attractive public space may stay empty if people have no reason to be there.\n\nThe most successful public spaces share a few features. First, they are easy to reach on foot from busy streets, rather than hidden behind buildings. Second, they offer different kinds of activity at the same time: a playground for children, a quiet corner for older people, a cafe, a stretch of grass. A space used by only one group tends to feel unsafe to everybody else. Third, they allow people to stay without spending money, which is why benches matter so much.\n\nJanet Byrne, who studies urban life, argues that the value of such places cannot be counted fully in economic terms. A good public space teaches a city how to behave, she says. Children meet adults who are not their parents; strangers learn to share a table; people who disagree about everything else still sit on the same bench. Her research in eight neighbourhoods found that residents who used a local square regularly were more likely to know their neighbours and to trust them.\n\nThat is not an argument for building a square everywhere. Some places need shops, housing or public transport more urgently. But when space is available, the cheapest improvements are often the most effective. A few benches, a working fountain and a rule that allows people to sit without buying anything can turn an empty area into the busiest part of a neighbourhood. Maintenance matters too: a broken fountain or a dirty bench quickly tells people that the space is not cared for."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","title":"大学英语四级标准模拟卷 10","total":22,"wordbank":["automatic","benefit","effective","eventually","force","gradually","likely","maintain","reminders","settle","similar","tracked","unexpected","whole","wrongly"],"materials":{"cloze":"Most of us believe that changing a habit depends on willpower, but research suggests something different. When people try to (26) into a new routine, the hardest part is usually the first three weeks. Scientists who (27) the behaviour of two hundred volunteers found that a habit is built when an action is repeated in the same place at the same time. The setting, rather than the effort, does much of the work. In one study, students who put (28) on their desks to drink more water were far more (29) to succeed than those who simply promised to try harder. The pattern was (30) in offices and in schools. Researchers therefore advise people to increase the difficulty only (31), and never to (32) themselves to change everything at once. A football beside the door, for example, is more useful than a lecture about exercise. After a few months the new action becomes (33), and the effort disappears. The real (34) is not a perfect week but a routine you can (35) even when you are tired or busy.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Queues that vanished\nAt half past twelve the main canteen used to look like a stadium entrance. Students queued for twenty minutes, ate in ten and complained for the rest of the afternoon. Since the university introduced app ordering last term, the queue has almost disappeared: you pay on your phone, walk to the collection shelf and pick up a box with your number on it. The canteen manager says it now serves about four hundred more meals a day with the same number of staff."},{"key":"B","text":"Robots behind the counter\nTwo machines have also appeared in the kitchen. One stirs large pots of soup, and the other fries eggs in a line, six at a time. The staff admit that the egg machine is faster than any of them, especially at breakfast. Head chef Zhou insists this is good news: the machines handle the boring work, while his team designs the weekly menu and talks to students. Nobody became a cook because they wanted to fry eight hundred eggs, he says."},{"key":"C","text":"What students miss\nNot every change has been welcomed. A small group of students has started a petition asking the university to keep at least one counter where a person serves you and asks how your day is going. Their argument is not about price or speed; it is about being treated as a person rather than a number. One of them told the campus paper that she lives alone, and that the cook who remembers her name is the only person she speaks to before noon."},{"key":"D","text":"Data on the menu\nBehind the scenes, the new system has produced a surprising amount of information. Managers can now see that the vegetarian dish is popular on Wednesdays, that soup sells badly on hot days and that food is often wasted on Friday evenings. This has changed the menu, and not always in the way students expected: portions are smaller, but the kitchen has cut waste by nearly a third and now buys vegetables from a local farm twice a week."},{"key":"E","text":"A cheaper way to run a canteen\nAutomation is often described as a way to save money, and here the numbers are honest. But the savings have come mostly from planning, not from replacing people. No one has been dismissed since the system arrived; two staff members have gone back to night school to learn data analysis and are now in charge of the ordering. The canteen also spends far less on food that nobody eats."},{"key":"F","text":"What comes next\nThe team is now testing something more ambitious: a menu that adjusts to each student's health data, offering a lighter lunch before a sports match and more iron to those who need it. The idea raises questions, since the university insists that health information stays private. For now the trial is voluntary, and only three hundred students have signed up. The results will be studied before any decision is made."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Heading to a hotel in central Singapore, a guest may be greeted by a machine about a metre tall. It can check in guests, carry their luggage to the lift and answer simple questions in four languages. Robots like this one, once a rare sight, are becoming ordinary in hotels, hospitals, restaurants and shopping centres. According to industry data, the number of service robots sold worldwide has more than tripled in five years, and the pandemic accelerated the trend: businesses that had lost staff to illness or quarantine needed help fast.\n\nSupporters point to clear advantages. Service robots work without breaks and handle repetitive tasks such as delivering medicine in hospitals or cleaning floors in shopping centres at night. In hospitals in Japan, robots carry meals and laundry, which saves nurses perhaps two hours a day, time that can be spent with patients. In some restaurants a small robot delivers dishes while the human staff talk to customers, which many managers say is a better use of their skills.\n\nYet the picture is not entirely bright. One survey of hotel guests found that although most people were curious about the robots, fewer than a third said they would trust one with a problem such as a lost booking. Others complained that asking a machine for advice felt cold and unhelpful. Cost is another barrier: a delivery robot may cost as much as a year's salary for a member of staff, and small businesses rarely have the money. Researchers in the field add a further warning. Robots work well when a task can be described step by step, and badly when the unexpected happens. A child running into the corridor or a confused elderly patient can stop a robot completely, and the solution usually arrives on two legs. The best results, these researchers say, come from teams in which robots do the carrying and people do the deciding."},{"key":"2","text":"For most of the twentieth century, the phrase learning a trade meant learning one skill well and using it for thirty years. Automation has made that model much less reliable. A machine can now handle the filing, the calculations and even some of the writing that once filled a working day, and the tasks it cannot yet handle change faster than any syllabus can be rewritten. The result is a quiet shift in what employers say they need.\n\nA survey of two thousand companies in Asia and Europe found that the three abilities most often named in job advertisements were not technical at all. They were the ability to communicate clearly, to solve unfamiliar problems and to learn something new quickly. Technical skills still matter, the report says, but they are no longer a guarantee of security; they are the entry ticket. What keeps a person employed is the capacity to add a second, related skill: a driver who learns to service electric vehicles, a nurse who learns to manage health data, a warehouse worker who becomes a robot maintenance assistant.\n\nSome governments have begun to design training around this idea. Instead of funding short courses in one occupation, they offer workers paid time to study for a certificate in a nearby field, and some companies have copied the scheme. The early results are encouraging but modest. In one programme, about sixty per cent of participants finished the course, and three quarters of those who finished were still in work a year later. The researchers who evaluated the programme noted one unexpected detail: workers who studied with a colleague were twice as likely to finish. The deeper lesson may be about schools rather than factories. Students who have learned how to learn, and who are not afraid of being beginners, adapt most easily. A curriculum that rewards the right answer above the honest question may be training people for a world that is already disappearing."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","title":"2023年12月大学英语四级真题风格卷（原创题面）","total":22,"wordbank":["almost","complete","expect","fixed","gradually","join","learn","likely","pleasure","prepare","rarely","share","simple","tired","whole"],"materials":{"cloze":"Few habits have changed as quickly as the family dinner. Thirty years ago most parents could (26) that everyone would be at the table by seven; today work, sport and screens make that (27) impossible. Sociologists who study family life are not surprised. What does surprise them is how much is lost when the shared meal disappears. Families that eat together at least four times a week (28) far more about each other's lives, and children in such families are (29) to do well at school. The meal also passes on culture: recipes, manners and stories travel from one generation to the next around a table, not through a screen. Some busy households now keep one (30) dinner a week and treat it as an appointment that cannot be moved. Others (31) the cooking among all members, so that nobody has to (32) a whole meal alone. Experts advise parents to keep the food plain and to let children (33) the conversation. A (34) dish cooked by a child may be the beginning of a lifelong habit. The point, they say, is not the food but the (35) of sitting down together.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"A late start, a real benefit\nResearchers who study the brain have good news for anyone over thirty who has always wanted to play an instrument. In several studies, adults who took up music in middle age showed improvements in memory and in the ability to follow two conversations at once. The changes were smaller than those seen in children who practise for years, but they appeared after only six months of weekly lessons. The researchers think the effort itself, rather than the result, is what keeps the brain busy."},{"key":"B","text":"The first month is the hardest\nAsk any teacher of adult beginners where students give up, and the answer is almost always the same: the fourth or fifth week. The first lesson is exciting, the second is confusing, and by the fourth the fingers hurt and the music sounds nothing like the recording. Teachers say the students who survive this stage are not the most talented ones but those who booked the lessons in advance and told a friend about them."},{"key":"C","text":"Practice in small pieces\nOne piece of advice appears in almost every method book for beginners: do not practise the whole piece. Divide it into sections of four or eight bars, play each one slowly, and join them only when every section is comfortable. Experienced players spend most of their practice time on the two or three bars that cause trouble, while beginners tend to start again from the beginning each time they make a mistake. The second approach feels like progress and is mostly a waste of time."},{"key":"D","text":"Finding the right teacher\nBefore choosing a teacher, adults should ask themselves what they actually want. Someone who dreams of playing in a band needs a teacher who will teach chords and rhythm; someone who loves classical music needs one who will correct the position of the hand. A good teacher asks about these goals in the first lesson. It is also worth asking whether the teacher has experience with adult learners, who need explanations and often want to know why."},{"key":"E","text":"Playing with others\nLearning alone is possible, especially with the help of videos, but the students who keep going are usually the ones who play with others. A local band, a church choir or a monthly gathering of amateur players gives the practice a purpose and a date. One group of adult beginners in Leeds meets in a pub every second Sunday; nobody is paid, nobody is judged, and the oldest member is eighty-one."},{"key":"F","text":"What it costs\nThe cost of learning an instrument is often exaggerated. A decent second-hand violin or keyboard can cost less than a mobile phone, and many libraries now lend instruments for a term. Lessons are the larger expense, but group classes and online sessions have brought prices down. Teachers point out that money spent on a forgotten instrument is the real waste, so it is sensible to rent before buying."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Not long ago, an audiobook meant a set of CDs borrowed from a library. Today it is the fastest-growing part of the publishing industry: sales have risen by double digits every year for a decade, and in some markets one book in five is now read through headphones. The reasons are practical. A commute, a gym session or a pile of washing-up becomes reading time, and listeners who had stopped buying paper books often become readers again.\n\nPublishers responded quickly, hiring actors and paying for studios and sound effects. Some well-known titles now have two versions, one in a single familiar voice and one with a full cast, and a few authors have begun to write with narration in mind, shortening long descriptions that sound slow when spoken. For language learners the format has an obvious attraction: the written text and its careful pronunciation are never far apart.\n\nNot everyone is delighted. Teachers who study reading worry that listening is a different skill. A reader who skips back to check a sentence is doing something a listener rarely does, and the attention demanded by a complicated argument may be lost when the voice continues without pause. A study of university students found that those who only listened remembered fewer details of a chapter than those who read it on paper, although they remembered the main idea equally well.\n\nThe most sensible view may be that the two forms do different jobs. Stories and interviews suit the ear, while a textbook or a contract still asks for the eye. What is striking, however, is that the argument itself has changed. A decade ago people asked whether listening counted as reading. Now they ask when it is the better choice."},{"key":"2","text":"The Rex was the only cinema in a town of nine thousand people, and when it closed in 2011 the building was sold and used for storing animal feed. For four years the town had nothing but the television in the pub. Then a retired teacher, Mary Appleton, asked at a public meeting whether people would pay five pounds a month to own a cinema. To her surprise, three hundred households said yes on the spot.\n\nThe Rex reopened two years later as a community cinema. It is run by a committee of twelve volunteers: a former electrician looks after the projector, two teenagers run the ticket desk on Saturdays, and Mary, now seventy-eight, chooses most of the films. The programme mixes new releases with old favourites, and once a month a local historian introduces a film connected with the area. Tickets are cheaper than in the city, and a small grant pays for free screenings for school classes.\n\nRunning a cinema is not easy. The projector broke down twice last year, and the committee spent a month deciding whether to spend nearly all its savings on a digital system. Membership has slipped slightly as streaming services have grown. Yet the audiences keep coming, and not always for the film. On a Friday night in winter, the Rex is one of the few warm, noisy places in town where people of different ages sit in the same room. Mary says that is the point. The films matter, she explains, but what people are really buying is an evening out with their neighbours."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","title":"2024年6月大学英语四级真题风格卷（原创题面）","total":22,"wordbank":["casual","careful","cheaper","closed","dramatic","flexible","never","offer","practical","quiet","reduced","similar","spacious","vacant","vividly"],"materials":{"cloze":"For many years the street market seemed to belong to the past. Supermarkets were bigger, cheaper and open every day, and by 2015 hundreds of town-centre markets had shut. Recently the picture has changed: in several cities a market building that had been left (26) for years now opens four days a week. What explains the change? Prices help. A stall has low rents, so fruit and bread are often (27) than in a large shop. Choice helps too: a market can (28) vegetables from growers within thirty kilometres that supermarkets refuse to stock. Perhaps the strongest reason, though, is social. A market fills the space where shops have (29), and it turns a street into a place where people talk. One survey found that shoppers stayed three times longer when a market was running. Councils have noticed. Some now offer a year of (30) rent, charged at half the usual rate, and (31) advice for new traders. The project is not without risk, since a market needs (32) management of stock, money and people, and one unsuccessful season can frighten traders away. But where the plan works, the effect is (33): residents stop driving to the supermarket outside town, and shops nearby sell more. Above all, a street that felt (34) begins to look alive, and people who had (35) met now greet each other by name.","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Lectures are not disappearing\nReports of the death of the lecture are premature. In most universities the large lecture hall is still used, and for good reasons. A single expert can introduce a whole field to four hundred students in an hour, and the shared experience of listening together is not easy to reproduce online. What has changed is the purpose: the lecture is now expected to introduce a topic and inspire interest, rather than to pass on information that students could read."},{"key":"B","text":"The flipped classroom\nThe most widespread change is the flipped classroom. Students read a chapter or watch a short video before the class, and the time with the teacher is used for problems, discussion and argument. Teachers who have tried it report that the first ten minutes are uncomfortable, because half the class has not prepared. After a few weeks, however, the proportion of prepared students rises sharply, and the questions become far better."},{"key":"C","text":"Recording everything\nAlmost every lecture is now recorded and posted online. The theory is attractive: students can pause, rewind and revise. The practice is more complicated. One study of engineering students found that although nearly everyone watched the recordings, most watched at double speed the night before the exam, and attendance in class fell by a third. The same study found that students who watched a recording twice and took notes did better than those who attended in person."},{"key":"D","text":"A new kind of homework\nHomework has changed shape as well. Instead of writing a summary of a lecture, students are now asked to prepare a question, find a counter-example or explain a concept to a classmate. Some courses use short online quizzes that must be completed before the class begins. The quizzes are worth very little, but the answers tell the teacher exactly which ideas need more time, information that a lecture alone never provides."},{"key":"E","text":"What students still need\nWhat students still need is harder to automate. They need someone to notice that they are struggling, to suggest a book that is not on the list, and to tell them honestly whether their essay is any good. Large lectures cannot do this, and neither can videos. Departments that have redesigned their courses usually say the same thing: the time saved by moving information online was most valuable when it was spent in small groups."},{"key":"F","text":"The cost of change\nNone of this is free. Preparing a good video takes a teacher perhaps eight hours, and redesigning a course can take a whole summer. Universities that expect the change to happen without support are often disappointed. A recent survey found that teachers who received help from a learning designer were twice as likely to say the new approach had improved their students' results."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Each autumn, thousands of students arrive at university straight from school, and a small but growing number arrive a year later, after a gap year. The idea of taking a year off between school and university used to worry parents, who imagined a year of sleeping late and playing computer games. Research suggests a different picture. In Britain, where the practice has been studied for decades, students who took a structured gap year, working, volunteering or travelling with a purpose, are slightly less likely to drop out and slightly more likely to finish with a good degree. The effect is small, and researchers are careful not to overstate it. Students who take a gap year are already, on average, more confident and better organized than those who do not, so it is hard to separate the year from the person.\n\nWhat the evidence does support is narrower. A year of doing something difficult, teaching in a rural school, working in a restaurant kitchen or learning a language badly before learning it better, gives young people a kind of confidence that classrooms rarely produce. They discover that they can be useful, that adults will listen to them, and that failure is survivable. They also learn what they do not want to do, which can be as valuable as discovering a career.\n\nFor families considering it, the practical advice from universities is simple. The year should have a plan, a purpose and someone to report back to; it does not need to be expensive or far away. A gap year spent well is not a year off. It is a year of a completely different kind of learning."},{"key":"2","text":"Ten years ago most shop owners on a British high street would have described the internet as a problem. Online shops were cheaper, took no rent and never closed, and one in eight high-street shops stood empty. The survivors have gradually discovered a different way to use the internet: not to sell, but to be found.\n\nThe most successful examples are surprisingly simple. A hardware shop in Sheffield became known across the country after the owner began filming two-minute videos of unusual repairs, such as mending an old lamp or a broken pram. The videos cost nothing and took a few minutes to make, but customers now travel from three neighbouring towns, and the shop sells more of the parts it once kept for years on the shelf. A bakery in a small town posts a photograph of the morning's bread each day at six, and its queue of regulars has grown. What these shops have in common is not a large budget but a habit: someone posts two or three times a week, in a voice that sounds like a person rather than a leaflet.\n\nThe change is not free. It takes an hour or two of an owner's evening, and owners who post for a month and then stop often report no benefit at all. Misleading reviews are another trouble; one shop now answers every review, good or bad, because the reply is read by the next hundred customers. Yet the shopkeepers interviewed for a recent report were almost all optimistic. As one of them put it, the internet cannot deliver a pair of boots to your door, but it can remind you which door to knock on."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","title":"2024年12月大学英语四级真题风格卷（原创题面）","total":22,"wordbank":[],"materials":{"cloze":"选词填空。阅读下面短文，从词库 A–O 中为 26–35 每个空白选出最合适的词，每个词只用一次，其中有五个词为多余选项。\n\nOnline shopping has changed the way people buy almost everything, and for many older people it has become a (26) ______ way to get daily necessities. Instead of carrying heavy bags home, they can place (27) ______ on a mobile app and have them (28) ______ to their door. However, not everyone finds the technology (29) ______ at first. Some seniors (30) ______ with small screens and passwords, and they may worry that their money is not safe. Community centres in several cities have started free classes to help them overcome these (31) ______. Volunteers teach them how to search for products, compare prices and pay safely. The results are encouraging: those who attend the classes (32) ______ become more confident, and many say the service has made them feel less (33) ______. For them, the greatest benefit is not speed but company, because a delivery worker's knock can be the only conversation of the day. Experts therefore suggest that companies should make their apps simpler and offer more (34) ______ support, especially for older customers who are less (35) ______ with digital tools than younger users.\n\n词库：A. access  B. barriers  C. comfortable  D. convenient  E. delivered  F. elderly  G. familiar  H. gradually  I. improve  J. isolated  K. orders  L. popular  M. reliable  N. struggle  O. technology","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Street lights that save energy. In one coastal city, thousands of street lamps now carry small sensors that measure movement and light. A lamp dims when the street is empty and brightens within a second when a cyclist or a pedestrian approaches. The system has cut the electricity used for lighting by almost a third, and residents say the streets still feel safe at night. City engineers add that the next step is to connect the lamps with traffic signals."},{"key":"B","text":"Medicine at a distance. In remote mountain towns, patients used to travel for hours to see a specialist. Now a small clinic in each town has a video screen, a camera and simple measuring devices, and a nurse helps the patient connect to a doctor in the city hospital. The doctor can look at test results, ask questions and send a prescription to a local pharmacy. Patients save a day of travelling, and hospital clinics are less crowded."},{"key":"C","text":"Bins that report their fill level. Rubbish bins in the city centre are now fitted with cheap sensors that send a message when they are nearly full. Instead of following a fixed route every day, collection trucks are sent only to the bins that need emptying, which has reduced fuel use and early-morning noise. The data also shows which streets produce the most waste, helping the city plan recycling campaigns."},{"key":"D","text":"Rides for older travellers. Many older residents find ordinary buses difficult: the stops are far apart, the steps are high, and the timetable is hard to read. A new service uses small electric shuttles that can be booked by a phone call. Drivers help passengers carry shopping and take them to clinics, markets and community centres for a fixed low fare. In its first six months the service carried more than ten thousand passengers."},{"key":"E","text":"Parks that cool themselves. A former car park in the city centre has been turned into a small park with trees, grass and a shallow water channel. On hot summer days the temperature inside the park is about four degrees lower than in the surrounding streets, and office workers come there at lunchtime to eat and rest. The city plans to build similar green spaces in the three districts that are most exposed to heat."},{"key":"F","text":"Repair shops for broken things. Once a month, a community hall opens its doors to people carrying broken lamps, kettles, toys and bicycles. Volunteer repairers fix what they can, free of charge, and explain what they are doing so that owners can learn to make simple repairs themselves. Last year the group kept more than two tonnes of small appliances out of the rubbish, and the waiting list for helpers is now longer than the list of broken objects."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Smartwatches are no longer just a way to check messages. Millions of people now use them to count their steps, measure their heart rate and track how well they sleep. Health authorities have welcomed the trend: when people can see their own data, the argument goes, they are more likely to take exercise and less likely to ignore warning signs.\n\nYet doctors are increasingly cautious about the numbers. A wrist device is not a medical instrument. Its heart rate readings can be disturbed by movement, its sleep measurement is based on guesswork, and its step count depends on how the device is worn. One study found that two popular models differed by nearly 15 percent when counting the same walk. More troubling is the effect on anxious users. Some check their heart rate dozens of times a day and are convinced that a minor change means serious illness, so they visit clinics that are already busy. Some doctors now ask such patients to bring a month of readings rather than a single number, which gives a fairer picture and saves time in the consultation.\n\nNone of this means that wearable devices are useless. They are good at showing long-term patterns. A slow fall in daily activity over several weeks can be a helpful signal for a doctor, and a simple reminder to stand up every hour may do more good than an expensive health plan. What matters is how the data is used. As one cardiologist put it, a watch can tell you that something has changed. It cannot tell you why, and it certainly cannot replace a conversation with a doctor."},{"key":"2","text":"Twenty years ago, bottled water seemed to be the future. Supermarkets devoted whole aisles to it, restaurants proudly poured it, and carrying a plastic bottle became a habit for commuters and students alike. Today the picture looks different. Across Europe and parts of Asia, cities are installing public refill stations and handing out reusable bottles, and a growing number of shops and cafes allow customers to fill up for free.\n\nThe argument for refill stations is partly about waste. A single plastic bottle may take hundreds of years to break down, and even when it is recycled, the process uses energy and water. Refill stations, by contrast, serve thousands of people from one pipe. Supporters also point to cost: tap water is far cheaper than the bottled kind, and they question whether the difference in taste is as great as advertising suggests. In blind tests, many volunteers cannot tell one from the other, and restaurants and offices have begun to install refill points of their own.\n\nBut the movement has its critics. Some consumers worry about the quality of tap water, especially in cities where the pipes are old. Others say that refill stations are only convenient for people who work or travel in the city centre; those living in distant suburbs may still find it easier to buy a bottle. For the habit to change, therefore, the stations must be almost everywhere a person goes: stations, parks, schools, offices and bus stops.\n\nThat is a practical challenge, but hardly an impossible one. The real test is not whether people approve of free water in theory, but whether a refill is easier than a purchase. Where that is true, the old habit tends to disappear."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","title":"2025年6月大学英语四级真题风格卷（原创题面）","total":22,"wordbank":[],"materials":{"cloze":"选词填空。阅读下面短文，从词库 A–O 中为 26–35 每个空白选出最合适的词，每个词只用一次，其中有五个词为多余选项。\n\nEvery day, university canteens across the country serve millions of meals, and a surprising (26) ______ of food ends up in the bin. A recent (27) ______ at one university found that students (28) ______ away about sixty grams of food per meal on average. Much of it had never been touched: rice left on the plate, vegetables pushed to one side, half a dish that was simply too large.\n\nThe reasons are not hard to find. When the (29) ______ offers only large dishes at a low price, people tend to take more than they can eat, and busy students are often not (30) ______ of how much they leave behind.\n\nSeveral universities are now trying to (31) ______ the problem. Some canteens offer smaller (32) ______ at a lower price, so that a light eater is not forced to waste food. Others put up posters and (33) ______ students to take (34) ______ home in (35) ______ boxes. Managers say the results are encouraging: in one canteen, waste fell by almost a third within a single term.\n\n词库：A. amount  B. aware  C. bins  D. consumers  E. encourage  F. entirely  G. leftovers  H. menu  I. portions  J. reduce  K. reusable  L. survey  M. throw  N. wasted  O. weight","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Cheaper tickets for everyone. Last year the city cut bus fares by a third and made travel free for students and older residents at weekends. Passenger numbers rose by almost 18 percent within six months, and shop owners in the suburbs say more customers now arrive by bus. The city has also introduced a single ticket that can be used on buses, trains and ferries for ninety minutes."},{"key":"B","text":"Buses after midnight. For years the last bus left the city centre at half past ten, leaving nurses, cleaners and restaurant staff to pay for taxis. A pilot late-night service now runs until one in the morning on Fridays and Saturdays, and a second route will follow next year. Drivers say the buses are busy but calm, and passengers say the service has changed their working lives."},{"key":"C","text":"Knowing when the bus will come. Real-time screens at two hundred stops show how many minutes remain until the next bus, and an app sends an alert when the vehicle is close. The city reports that complaints about waiting fell by a third in the first year. For passengers, the gain is not speed but certainty: knowing that the bus is four minutes away makes standing in the rain far easier."},{"key":"D","text":"Bikes welcome on board. New buses carry a rack that holds two bicycles at the front, and several train lines now offer a carriage with hooks and charging points for e-bikes. Commuters can cycle to the station, take the bike on the train and finish the journey on two wheels. The number of people combining a bike with public transport has doubled since the facilities appeared."},{"key":"E","text":"A quiet corner on the train. One railway company has marked the first two carriages of every train as quiet zones, where phone calls are not allowed, announcements are reduced and the lights are slightly dimmed. Passengers use them to work, read or sleep through the journey. A survey found that eight out of ten travellers wanted the zones extended, even those who rarely used them."},{"key":"F","text":"Parking at the edge of town. Drivers from outside the city can leave their cars free of charge in a large car park beside the motorway and take a bus into the centre every ten minutes. The car park is full by eight in the morning, and the number of cars entering the city centre has fallen by a fifth, which has improved air quality in the narrow streets."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Ask people to describe their ideal day and few will mention boredom. We treat it as a failure of planning, something to be removed with a phone, a game or another cup of coffee. Yet a growing number of psychologists argue that boredom is not an enemy at all. It may even be useful.\n\nIn a well-known experiment, volunteers were asked to spend fifteen minutes copying numbers from a telephone directory. Afterwards, they were given a test of creativity: they had to suggest as many uses as possible for a pair of plastic cups. The bored group produced far more original ideas than a control group who had spent the same time doing something pleasant. The researchers believe that when the mind is under-stimulated, it starts to search for new directions, connecting memories and ideas in ways that a busy mind cannot.\n\nBoredom also sends a signal. Just as hunger tells us to eat, boredom tells us that what we are doing is not meaningful, and it pushes us to change something: to call a friend, start a project or take a walk. People who never allow themselves to be bored may lose that signal, and with it the chance to notice what really matters to them.\n\nThe modern world, however, makes boredom almost impossible. Every spare minute can be filled with messages, videos and shopping. Some teachers therefore ask students to leave their phones at home for a day, and many report that the first hours are uncomfortable but that conversations become longer and homework gets finished faster. Perhaps the skill we need most is not entertainment, but the ability to sit with an empty moment and see what it brings."},{"key":"2","text":"Street food is one of the joys of city life: a hot bowl of noodles, a paper cone of fried chicken or a warm pancake eaten while walking. For many people it is also cheaper than a restaurant, and for small business owners it offers a way to start trading with very little capital. For a visitor, it is also the quickest way to taste what a city really eats.\n\nYet street food has long had a reputation problem. Because stalls are small and often temporary, health inspectors find it difficult to check every one, and customers cannot see what happens behind the counter. In some cities, the answer has been to ban street selling altogether, but this usually drives vendors into less visible places, where conditions may be even worse.\n\nA number of cities are now trying a different approach. Instead of closing markets, they are organised. Vendors are given a fixed pitch with clean water and rubbish collection, in exchange for paying a small fee and following basic rules such as storing food at the correct temperature and covering it. Some markets train their own members and publish inspection results on a notice board, so that a stall that loses customers will think twice about food safety.\n\nThe early results are positive. In one city, complaints about food safety fell by half after the first year, while the number of stalls rose, which suggests that customers and sellers both benefit. The lesson is that street food does not have to choose between charm and hygiene. With a little organisation, a city can keep its lively streets and its safe meals."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","title":"2025年12月大学英语四级真题风格卷（原创题面）","total":22,"wordbank":[],"materials":{"cloze":"选词填空。阅读下面短文，从词库 A–O 中为 26–35 每个空白选出最合适的词，每个词只用一次，其中有五个词为多余选项。\n\nMost students now bring a laptop to lectures, and typing looks like an efficient way to capture everything the lecturer says. A (26) ______ number of studies, however, suggests that writing notes by hand may help learners understand better. In one experiment, students watched the same short lecture in two groups: one typed their notes, the other wrote them on paper. The typed notes were almost (27) ______, because students could type faster. Yet when everyone took a test a week later, the handwritten group performed (28) ______, remembering more of the (29) ______ points.\n\nOne explanation is that writing by hand is slower, so students must (30) ______ what matters. They cannot copy every word, which forces them to (31) ______ the information in their own words. This processing supports memory. Typing, by contrast, encourages a (32) ______ of transcription: the words pass from the lecturer's mouth into the notebook without ever being (33) ______.\n\nThe lesson is not that laptops are useless. They are helpful for searching for resources or for group work. For lectures, though, students may (34) ______ more if they put the keyboard away and (35) ______ a pen instead.\n\n词库：A. benefit  B. better  C. complete  D. decide  E. growing  F. kind  G. key  H. understood  I. use  J. summarise  K. process  L. repeat  M. simply  N. therefore  O. widely","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"Peer tutoring in the library. Every weekday evening, two floors of the main library are set aside for peer tutoring. Second- and third-year students who did well in a subject offer free hour-long sessions to first-year students. No appointment is needed: a student simply checks the timetable, arrives with questions and joins a small group. Last year more than two thousand first-year students used the service, and most said it helped them adjust to university-level work."},{"key":"B","text":"Study rooms open until midnight. During the exam period, the university keeps three study rooms open until midnight, and from this term they will stay open all year. Each room has movable tables, fast internet and a small kitchen. Students book a slot online, and a monitor makes sure that groups do not occupy a room without using it. The rooms are designed for group discussion rather than silent study."},{"key":"C","text":"Subject guides on the library website. The library website hosts more than three hundred subject guides, written by librarians who know each field. A guide lists the best databases, key journals and a short video on how to search them. They are free and always available, yet only a small number of students know that they exist. The library is now linking each guide to the relevant course page."},{"key":"D","text":"Equipment training for researchers. Anyone planning to use the research equipment in the science building must first attend an afternoon of training. Students learn how to book a machine, how to prepare a sample and what to do in an emergency. After the training they receive a card that allows them into the laboratory. The department says the training has cut accidents to almost zero and has made booking far fairer."},{"key":"E","text":"Careers advice from the first year. Most students visit the careers service only in their final month. The office is trying to change that by offering first-year workshops on writing a CV, finding internships and talking to employers. Advisers are available for individual appointments, and a notice board lists part-time jobs and summer projects. The service is not only about finding a job; it also helps students see what their course can lead to."},{"key":"F","text":"Small research grants for students. A small fund pays for students to carry out their own research projects. Groups can apply for up to five thousand yuan to buy materials, travel to a site or attend a conference. The application is short, and a supervisor must agree to support the project. Last year thirty projects were funded, including a study of local birds and an experiment on low-cost water filters."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"At home, a typical student may be reading a course text, replying to messages and half-watching a video, all at the same time. People who do this often describe themselves as good at multitasking. Research tells a different story. When the brain switches between two tasks, it does not process both at once; it moves its attention from one to the other, and each switch costs a little time. The effect is small when switches are rare, but it adds up when messages arrive every few minutes.\n\nIn one study, students were asked to read a chapter while receiving short messages. Compared with a quiet group, they took about a quarter longer to finish and later remembered less of what they had read. Another line of research found that students who used their phones during lectures took notes of a lower quality and were more likely to miss the connection between ideas.\n\nThere is a trap here. Because messages are brief, students feel that they have lost only a few seconds. In fact, they must rebuild their understanding of the text each time they return to it, and that rebuilding is the expensive part. Some researchers call this attention residue: part of the mind remains on the conversation even after the phone is put away.\n\nExperiments with simpler solutions have been encouraging. Students who left their phones in another room, or who turned off notifications for a single hour, reported no great difficulty and finished their work faster. The lesson is not that technology is harmful, but that attention is limited. It deserves the same care that we give to any other valuable resource."},{"key":"2","text":"Ask an urban planner what makes a neighbourhood pleasant, and the answer is often a single number: fifteen. The idea of the fifteen-minute city is that people should be able to reach most of what they need, such as shops, schools, a clinic, a park and a place to meet friends, within a quarter of an hour on foot or by bicycle. The concept is not new; many older towns grew up this way before cars became common. What is new is how seriously some cities now take it when planning new districts.\n\nThe benefits sound obvious, but they are worth stating. Streets with fewer cars are quieter and safer, and children who can walk to school are healthier than those who are driven. Small local shops survive when residents pass them daily, and older people who can reach a clinic on foot keep their independence longer. The environment gains too: short car journeys are the most polluting, so replacing them with walking or cycling cuts both emissions and noise.\n\nBringing the idea to an existing city is harder. Shops may have already moved to large shopping centres on the edge of town, and office workers may live far from their workplaces. Planners therefore talk about a network of local centres rather than a single perfect district: a few shops here, a clinic there, a good bus link between them. The aim is to make the ordinary journey to the shop or the school one that a person can imagine walking, rather than one that requires a car key.\n\nCities that have tried it report that the change is gradual. People do not switch to walking because a plan tells them to; they switch because the walk is pleasant, safe and convenient. Design, in the end, shapes habit."}]}},{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","title":"2026年6月大学英语四级真题风格卷（原创题面）","total":22,"wordbank":[],"materials":{"cloze":"选词填空。阅读下面短文，从词库 A–O 中为 26–35 每个空白选出最合适的词，每个词只用一次，其中有五个词为多余选项。\n\nArriving in a research lab for the first time, many undergraduates imagine that they will spend most of their time making exciting discoveries. In practice, the first weeks are more about observation. A good supervisor will ask new students to (26) ______ everything they do, from the temperature of a water bath to the strange noise a machine makes. Keeping a careful record may feel boring, but it is the only way to (27) ______ an unexpected result later. Beginners also need to accept that experiments often fail. Rather than hiding a failed attempt, lab members discuss it at weekly meetings, where a problem that seems unusual to one student may be (28) ______ to someone else. With time, students learn to (29) ______ their methods when the data do not make sense. They also discover that asking a short question early can (30) ______ hours of wasted work. Patience is another lesson: some measurements need weeks before any pattern becomes (31) ______. For those who stay, the work brings a quiet (32) ______ that no single finished result can offer. Many students say the experience (33) ______ their idea of what science really is. They come to see that confidence grows slowly, and that the most valuable habit in a lab is not speed but (34) ______. By the end of a semester, most of them can (35) ______ their own small project from start to finish.\n\n词库：A. adjust  B. available  C. changed  D. complete  E. document  F. explain  G. familiar  H. gradually  I. measure  J. obvious  K. patience  L. prevent  M. replace  N. satisfaction  O. scientific","matching":[{"key":"A","text":"An idea that started small. The campus science festival began with a complaint. Three second-year students were tired of hearing that science was only for people in white coats, so they asked the student union for a small room, two tables and permission to put up posters. Their first event lasted one afternoon and attracted about forty visitors. Nobody expected it to become an annual tradition, least of all the organisers themselves."},{"key":"B","text":"Talks that anyone could follow. Every speaker at the festival is given the same instruction: imagine that your audience has never studied your subject. Students practise explaining their work in short talks, and a group of volunteers listens to early versions and points out every technical term that needs translating. The result is a programme that a first-year student, a parent or a school child can all follow without feeling lost."},{"key":"C","text":"Hands-on workshops. Listening is only part of the experience. In the workshops, visitors build simple circuits, look at cells under a microscope and test the strength of paper bridges. Materials are cheap and the experiments can be repeated at home. The organisers say that people remember a principle much better after their own hands have made something go wrong and then right again."},{"key":"D","text":"The student volunteers. More than eighty students take part in running the event, from designing posters to directing visitors. None of them is paid, but volunteers receive training in communication and a certificate that records their hours. Many say the work has helped them explain their own subject more clearly, and some have discovered that they enjoy teaching more than they expected."},{"key":"E","text":"Money matters. A festival of this size is not free to run. The organisers apply for small grants from the university, borrow equipment from departments and ask local businesses for support. Tickets, however, stay free, because the team believes that a price, even a small one, would keep some families away. Their rule is simple: every activity must be cheap enough to repeat next year."},{"key":"F","text":"Looking ahead. Next year the team hopes to invite schools from outside the city and to add a session for teachers. They are also discussing a travelling version that could visit smaller campuses. The plan is deliberately modest: a second day, a few more workshops, and a clear record of what worked, so that the festival can survive after its current organisers graduate."}],"reading":[{"key":"1","text":"Three years ago, the science magazine of a small university nearly closed. Its editor had graduated, the printer had raised its prices, and only four students remained on the team. Rather than let the magazine disappear, the four decided to change it. They stopped printing long articles written by professors and began to publish short pieces by undergraduates about research taking place on campus. The first issue of the new format was thirty pages long and included an interview with a student who had spent the summer counting insects in a city park.\nThe change worked. Readers said they finally understood what their classmates were working on, and students who had never written anything for a general audience discovered that explaining their subject was harder than they had imagined. A chemistry student described how a paragraph about her project went through five drafts before her flatmate could follow it. That experience, she said, was more useful than any writing course.\nThe magazine is now published twice a term, with a print run of eight hundred copies, and it has a small budget from the student union. The editor is careful not to call it a research journal. Its purpose, she says, is not to record science but to start conversations: a reader who finishes an article should have a question to ask the person sitting next to them.\nNot every issue succeeds. Some topics attract few readers, and the team has learned to accept that. What matters is that a magazine which almost closed now has a waiting list of students who want to write for it, and that the science on campus is no longer invisible."},{"key":"2","text":"Every autumn, the international office at a British university runs a short course that has nothing to do with engineering or literature. It is called Academic Etiquette, and its purpose is to explain the unwritten rules of university life: how to address a professor, how to join a discussion without interrupting, and how to write an email that will actually be answered.\nThe email lesson is the most popular. Students bring messages they have already sent, and the class reads them together. Two versions appear again and again. One begins with no greeting and no subject line and asks five questions at once. The other begins with a long apology for troubling the professor, followed by a paragraph about the student's entire academic history, before the real request appears in the final line. Neither is likely to receive a quick reply.\nThe teacher offers a simple structure: a subject line that says exactly what the message is about, a brief greeting, the request in the first two sentences, and a closing that thanks the reader for their time. She also reminds students that professors receive hundreds of messages a week, and that a short, clear email is a sign of respect rather than a sign of laziness.\nAt the end of the course, students are asked to rewrite one of their own messages. The improvement is usually obvious. But the teacher insists that the deeper lesson is not about language. It is about understanding that academic life has its own culture, with habits and expectations that nobody explains in class. Learning to read that culture, she says, is part of learning the subject itself."}]}}];
window.CONTENT.questions = [
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"26. Typing lets a student record almost every word, but this can actually ____ understanding.","options":[],"answer":"hinder","explanation":"选 I. hinder。hinder 意为“阻碍、妨碍”，hinder understanding 指反而妨碍理解；benefit 虽也可接宾语，但与句中 but 的转折逻辑相反。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"27. When people write slowly, they must decide what matters, which makes the material easier to ____ later.","options":[],"answer":"recall","explanation":"选 L. recall。recall 意为“回想起、忆起”，与后面的 later 呼应，说明用自己的话记笔记能帮助日后回忆内容。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"28. The two groups scored ____ on simple factual questions.","options":[],"answer":"equally","explanation":"选 E. equally。空格修饰动词 scored，需要程度副词；equally 表示两组在简单事实题上“得分相同”，与 although 引导的让步关系一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"29. Handwriting also seems to keep the mind from ____ during long lectures.","options":[],"answer":"wandering","explanation":"选 O. wandering。keep sb from doing sth 为固定结构，wandering 指“走神、思绪游移”，说明手写让人无法不假思索地抄写，从而保持专注。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"30. Of course, laptops remain ____ for courses with large amounts of data.","options":[],"answer":"useful","explanation":"选 N. useful。remain 后接形容词作表语，useful 表示笔记本电脑在数据量大的课程中仍然“有用”，与前面的转折语气相符。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"31. No teacher should ____ students who genuinely need them.","options":[],"answer":"forbid","explanation":"选 G. forbid。forbid sb to do sth 意为“禁止某人做某事”，此处指不应禁止确实需要笔记本电脑的学生使用它。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"32. The ____ message is that the method matters less than the thinking behind it.","options":[],"answer":"general","explanation":"选 H. general。the general message 意为“总的信息、总体结论”，空格需形容词修饰 message，概括前文的对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"33. Teachers can help by ____ fewer but better questions.","options":[],"answer":"asking","explanation":"选 A. asking。by 后接动名词，ask questions 意为“提问”，fewer but better questions 说明问题应少而精。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"34. Teachers can also give students time after class to ____ their notes with a partner.","options":[],"answer":"compare","explanation":"选 C. compare。compare A with B 意为“把 A 与 B 作比较”，与 with a partner 搭配，指课后和同伴对照、补充笔记。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"blank","stem":"35. Good notes are not a ____ of the lecture but a record of the student's own ideas.","options":[],"answer":"copy","explanation":"选 D. copy。a copy of the lecture 意为“讲义的复制品”，与后面的 a record of the student's own ideas 形成对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"Donated books are examined and sorted into groups before they are sent out.","options":["Where the books come from","Sorting and checking","Choosing the right schools","Reaching remote areas","Training local teachers","The difference it makes"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段说明志愿者先检查 each book for missing pages, water damage and writing，再把 the rest 分成 categories，与题干“先检查再分类”一致，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"Some books have to be carried by motorbike where lorries cannot reach.","options":["Where the books come from","Sorting and checking","Choosing the right schools","Reaching remote areas","Training local teachers","The difference it makes"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段提到 In those places the boxes are transferred to motorbikes, which carry two at a time up the hills，与题干“卡车到不了的地方改用摩托车”直接对应，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"The charity asks each school which titles and subjects its students actually need.","options":["Where the books come from","Sorting and checking","Choosing the right schools","Reaching remote areas","Training local teachers","The difference it makes"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段说 staff contact the school ... They ask the head teacher how many students there are, what ages they teach and which subjects need materials，即先了解学校的实际需求，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"Books come from families, schools and publishers rather than being bought new.","options":["Where the books come from","Sorting and checking","Choosing the right schools","Reaching remote areas","Training local teachers","The difference it makes"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段说明书来自 families whose children have grown up、schools replacing their library stock 以及 a few publishers，即旧书主要由家庭、学校和出版社捐赠，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"Teachers are given training so that they can use the books in their lessons.","options":["Where the books come from","Sorting and checking","Choosing the right schools","Reaching remote areas","Training local teachers","The difference it makes"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E 段说 two trainers spend a week with the teachers, showing them how to run a reading club and how to read aloud，即教师会接受如何使用图书的培训，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What was the main purpose of university libraries in the past?","options":["To protect books and keep the collection safe.","To provide group study rooms for students.","To lend cameras and laptops.","To offer workshops on research skills."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说 the university library was a quiet building whose main purpose was to keep books safe，即过去大学图书馆的主要目的是保护藏书的安全，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What change have many modern university libraries made?","options":["They have built more silent reading rooms.","They have replaced some shelves with group study areas.","They have stopped lending printed books.","They have closed the cafés inside them."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段提到 Many libraries have moved the shelves to make room for group study areas, computer rooms and small studios，即把书架移开腾出小组学习区等空间，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do some older academics complain about?","options":["Libraries are too quiet for group work.","Students borrow too many books.","Silent reading rooms have gone and students distract each other.","Librarians ask too many questions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段说 Older academics sometimes complain that a library without silent reading rooms is no longer a library at all, and that students who work in groups distract those who need quiet，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the recent report suggest about successful libraries?","options":["They should own more printed volumes.","They should keep students quiet at all times.","They should close social spaces during exams.","They should offer both silent and social spaces."],"answer":"D","explanation":"最后一段引述报告说 the most successful libraries ... offer both worlds: a silent floor for those who want it, and lively spaces for those who need to work together，即兼顾安静与社交空间，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What do researchers mean by deep reading?","options":["Following a writer's argument and pausing over sentences.","Reading as many short messages as possible.","Listening to audiobooks while working.","Skimming a book to find facts quickly."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段解释 deep reading 指 a person follows an argument, notices the writer's tone and pauses over a sentence that seems especially well made，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the author's view of digital devices?","options":["They have destroyed the habit of reading.","They are not the enemy and can support deep reading.","They should not be used by university students.","They are only useful for short messages."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段明确说 Digital devices are not the enemy，并举出在平板上深读、用有声书重读书本等例子，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-01","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What do students learn from the short courses on reading habits?","options":["Reading in the morning is always boring.","Audiobooks are better than printed books.","A short period of full attention is worth more than hours of half-attention.","Books should be read as quickly as possible."],"answer":"C","explanation":"最后一段说学生发现 thirty minutes of concentration in the morning is worth more than three hours of half-attention，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"26. Films work best as a language-learning method when they are used with some ____.","options":[],"answer":"planning","explanation":"选 H. planning。with some planning 意为“有所计划”，与后文“不要只是打开母语字幕看故事”形成对比，空格需名词。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"27. On the second viewing, the brain is forced to ____ the sound of words with their meaning.","options":[],"answer":"connect","explanation":"选 B. connect。connect A with B 意为“把 A 与 B 联系起来”，指把词的读音与意义对应起来，是语言学习的常用搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"28. It also helps to ____ difficult expressions in a notebook and look them up afterwards.","options":[],"answer":"record","explanation":"选 J. record。record ... in a notebook 意为“把……记在笔记本里”，与 look them up afterwards 前后衔接；retain 强调记住，不接 in a notebook。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"29. Watching one film again after a week may be more ____ than watching five new films in a row.","options":[],"answer":"effective","explanation":"选 C. effective。more effective than 为比较级结构，effective 意为“有效的”，说明重复看同一部电影的学习效果可能更好。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"30. Teachers warn that films should not ____ other kinds of practice.","options":[],"answer":"replace","explanation":"选 K. replace。replace 意为“取代”，句中说明电影不应取代说、读、写等其他形式的练习，与后文 Speaking, reading and writing still need time 呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"31. Used ____, films can bring the language to life and remind learners why they are studying.","options":[],"answer":"wisely","explanation":"选 O. wisely。used wisely 为过去分词短语作状语，意为“如果使用得当”，需要副词修饰 used。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"32. The ____ of the story gives the brain something to hold on to.","options":[],"answer":"plot","explanation":"选 I. plot。the plot of the story 意为“故事情节”，情节为大脑提供线索，使新词更容易记住。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"33. A strong story makes new words easier to ____.","options":[],"answer":"retain","explanation":"选 L. retain。retain 意为“记住、保持”，easier to retain 表示新词更容易记住；record 强调记录动作，与“记住”语义不同。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"34. Films are not a ____ for hard work.","options":[],"answer":"substitute","explanation":"选 M. substitute。a substitute for sth 意为“某物的替代品”，此处说明电影不能代替刻苦学习。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"blank","stem":"35. Films are a ____ that makes the work more pleasant.","options":[],"answer":"tool","explanation":"选 N. tool。a tool that makes the work more pleasant 意为“一种让学习更愉快的工具”，与前半句 not a substitute 形成对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"New learners first practise simple actions such as switching the phone on and making a call.","options":["A growing need","Meeting in the library","Starting with the basics","One-to-one help","Safety and scams","Small changes, big effects"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段说 The first lesson is always the same. Learners practise switching the phone on and off, making a call, sending a short message，与题干“先练简单操作”一致，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"Learners are warned not to give bank details to strangers who call or send messages.","options":["A growing need","Meeting in the library","Starting with the basics","One-to-one help","Safety and scams","Small changes, big effects"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E 段专门讲防诈骗，提到 how to recognise a suspicious message, why they should never share a password or bank details，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"More and more older people need help as everyday services move online.","options":["A growing need","Meeting in the library","Starting with the basics","One-to-one help","Safety and scams","Small changes, big effects"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段指出 many services have moved onto the internet and left many older people behind，并说 calls for help have doubled in three years，与题干对应，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"Each learner is paired with one student who can go at the learner's own speed.","options":["A growing need","Meeting in the library","Starting with the basics","One-to-one help","Safety and scams","Small changes, big effects"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段说 Each learner works with the same student volunteer every week ... the volunteer learns what the person already knows，即固定的一对一帮助，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"Volunteers hold regular sessions in a quiet room of the local library.","options":["A growing need","Meeting in the library","Starting with the basics","One-to-one help","Safety and scams","Small changes, big effects"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段说 The sessions take place on Tuesday and Friday mornings in a quiet room at the back of the town library，与题干时间地点完全对应，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What advantage of AI tutoring tools is mentioned?","options":["They can give immediate feedback at any time.","They replace the need for human teachers.","They are always cheaper than books.","They help students make friends online."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说 AI 工具 can ... give a student immediate feedback at midnight，即为学生随时提供即时反馈，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do the studies of basic mathematics and language practice suggest?","options":["Students should use AI for very long sessions.","Regular short sessions with such tools improve test scores.","AI tools are useless for language learning.","Test scores fall when AI tools are used."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第一段提到 students who use such tools regularly do improve their test scores, especially when the tool is used for short, focused sessions，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What is the quieter worry mentioned in the passage?","options":["Students may become too polite.","AI tools may cost too much money.","Students may never experience the struggle that teaches them to think.","Teachers may lose their jobs immediately."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说 if a program always supplies the next step, students may never experience the struggle that teaches them how to think，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What line do most experts draw?","options":["AI should replace teachers in large classes.","AI should be used only in mathematics.","AI should be banned from schools.","AI can explain, test and remind, but the teacher must stay in charge."],"answer":"D","explanation":"末段说 AI can explain, test and remind; it cannot care whether the student understands，并强调 the machine is useful only if the teacher is in charge，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) How does the author describe the way many students concentrate today?","options":["In shorter bursts with rapid switching.","For hours without any interruption.","Only in the morning.","With the help of AI tools."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说 they concentrate in shorter bursts，并描述 a pattern of rapid switching，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the cost of an interruption according to the passage?","options":["It makes the screen brighter.","The mind needs several minutes to return to where it stopped.","It improves memory of the essay.","It saves time in the long run."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段说 After an interruption, the mind needs time to return to the point where it stopped, and some studies estimate that this recovery takes several minutes，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-02","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What did experiments with phones in another room find?","options":["Students worked more slowly.","Students forgot what they had read.","Students completed tasks faster and remembered more.","Students refused to work at all."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第四段说 Experiments ... found that they completed tasks faster and remembered more，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"26. A T-shirt may be worn a few times before it is ____ away.","options":[],"answer":"thrown","explanation":"选 M. thrown。be thrown away 意为“被扔掉”，与上衣只穿几次就被丢弃的语境一致，空格需过去分词构成被动。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"27. Some companies have begun to ____ the problem by collecting old clothes in their shops.","options":[],"answer":"tackle","explanation":"选 L. tackle。tackle the problem 意为“应对、处理问题”，与 by collecting old clothes 的方式说明相搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"28. Consumers have more ____ than they realise.","options":[],"answer":"power","explanation":"选 H. power。have more power than they realise 意为“拥有比自己意识到的更大的影响力”，指消费者可以通过选择影响环境。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"29. Washing clothes at a lower temperature and drying them in the air can ____ their life by years.","options":[],"answer":"extend","explanation":"选 F. extend。extend 意为“延长”，extend the life of clothes by years 即“把衣服的寿命延长数年”。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"30. Buying ____, well-made clothes costs more at first but saves money later.","options":[],"answer":"durable","explanation":"选 B. durable。durable 意为“耐穿的、结实的”，与 well-made 并列修饰 clothes；虽然初期花费更高，但此后更省钱。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"31. At swap events, friends bring clothes they no longer wear and take home something ____.","options":[],"answer":"different","explanation":"选 A. different。something different 意为“不一样的东西”，说明交换活动让旧衣换成别的衣物，减少购买。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"32. None of these actions is ____ on its own.","options":[],"answer":"enough","explanation":"选 D. enough。none of these actions is enough on its own 意为“单靠其中任何一项都不够”，与 but 后的 together 形成对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"33. Together these actions ____ the pressure on the planet.","options":[],"answer":"reduce","explanation":"选 J. reduce。reduce the pressure on the planet 意为“减轻对地球的压力”，是常见的动宾搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"34. The most ____ change, campaigners argue, is to buy less.","options":[],"answer":"effective","explanation":"选 C. effective。the most effective change 意为“最有效的改变”，形容词最高级修饰 change，表示倡导者认为最管用的做法。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"blank","stem":"35. The final advice is to buy less and choose ____.","options":[],"answer":"wisely","explanation":"选 N. wisely。choose wisely 意为“明智地选择”，需要副词修饰动词 choose，与 buy less 并列说明消费态度。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"A green roof can slow the flow of rainwater into drains during heavy storms.","options":["What a green roof is","Cooling the city","Holding back rainwater","A home for wildlife","The cost question","Examples around the world"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段说 A green roof slows the water down ... runs off more slowly，并提到它能 reduce the peak load on a system during the worst hour of a storm，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"These roofs offer food and shelter to birds and insects in crowded cities.","options":["What a green roof is","Cooling the city","Holding back rainwater","A home for wildlife","The cost question","Examples around the world"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段说 Green roofs create small islands of habitat above the traffic，并提到研究记录了 bees, beetles and nesting birds，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"A green roof is a layer of plants and soil placed on top of a building.","options":["What a green roof is","Cooling the city","Holding back rainwater","A home for wildlife","The cost question","Examples around the world"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段开头即说 A green roof is exactly what its name suggests: a building whose flat top has been covered with soil and plants，并介绍了下方的防水层、排水层等，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"The idea is now required by law in some cities and supported with money in others.","options":["What a green roof is","Cooling the city","Holding back rainwater","A home for wildlife","The cost question","Examples around the world"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F 段说 In Singapore, new buildings are required to include planting on their roofs，又提到 In Germany ... several cities offer financial support，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"Plants reduce the heat that buildings absorb and release into the streets.","options":["What a green roof is","Cooling the city","Holding back rainwater","A home for wildlife","The cost question","Examples around the world"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段解释植物 take up water and release it as vapour, a process that cools the air，并说绿色屋顶表面温度可比普通屋顶低三十度，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did one university achieve by replacing library lights with sensors?","options":["It served smaller meals in the dining hall.","It cut the building's electricity use by a fifth.","It built cycle lanes on campus.","It opened a repair workshop."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第一段说 One university replaced the lights in its library with sensors ... which cut electricity use in the building by a fifth，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why did food waste fall at another university?","options":["Students were given free bus passes.","The university built a garden behind the science building.","Dining halls served smaller portions at lower prices.","Students cooked for themselves."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第一段提到 Another began serving smaller portions in its dining halls and charging less for them, and found that food waste fell by almost a third，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the survey of twelve campuses suggest?","options":["Large official targets work best.","Technology is the key to change.","Students ignore projects started by societies.","Small, visible projects that are easy to join are most successful."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段说 A survey of twelve campuses found that the most successful projects were small, visible and easy to join，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do the researchers conclude?","options":["Change starts when students change one habit at a time.","Students should rethink their whole lifestyle.","Big targets are the only way to succeed.","Campuses should be treated as cities."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说 a student is more likely to change one habit than to rethink an entire lifestyle. That, the researchers conclude, is how change actually starts，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What was expected of bike-sharing schemes?","options":["They would replace public libraries.","They would pay for themselves immediately.","They would reduce traffic and pollution and improve health.","They would be used mainly by children."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第一段说 They would reduce traffic, cut pollution, improve public health and fill the gaps between bus stops and homes，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What problem appeared after the first months?","options":["Bicycles were too expensive to make.","Too few people wanted to cycle.","Cities ran out of buses.","Bicycles were badly parked, thrown into rivers and piled outside stations."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段说 Bicycles were left in the middle of pavements, thrown into rivers and piled outside railway stations，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-03","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does the author compare a shared bicycle to?","options":["A private car.","A public library that needs care.","A railway station.","A competition between companies."],"answer":"B","explanation":"末段说 a vehicle shared by thousands of people needs the same care as a public library: someone must check it, clean it and put it back in the right place，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"26. In the past, planners often ____ that old structures were simply in the way of progress.","options":[],"answer":"claimed","explanation":"选 D. claimed（对应第 26 空）。空格需填过去式动词，claim that... 表示‘声称’，与 In the past 的时态一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"27. Today a different view is spreading, and heritage protection has become an ____ part of urban planning.","options":[],"answer":"essential","explanation":"选 F. essential（对应第 27 空）。an essential part of 意为‘……不可或缺的一部分’，与‘观念正在传播’的语境一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"28. A stone arch or a wooden roof may need months of work by specialists whose skills are in short ____.","options":[],"answer":"supply","explanation":"选 N. supply（对应第 28 空）。in short supply 是固定搭配，意为‘供不应求、十分稀缺’，说明掌握这类技艺的专家很少。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"29. Once a building is gone, the stories attached to it are ____ as well.","options":[],"answer":"forgotten","explanation":"选 G. forgotten（对应第 29 空）。are forgotten 为被动语态，与前半句 Once a building is gone 对应，表示故事也随之被遗忘。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"30. Some cities have found ____ solutions.","options":[],"answer":"creative","explanation":"选 E. creative（对应第 30 空）。下文把旧纺织厂改成图书馆即为‘有创意的’做法，creative 修饰 solutions 最贴切。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"31. Tourists come, but the ____ visitors are local people who use the reading rooms every day.","options":[],"answer":"regular","explanation":"选 L. regular（对应第 31 空）。regular visitors 指‘常客’，与后文‘每天都来阅览室’呼应，并与游客形成对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"32. The project also provided ____ work for craftsmen who might otherwise have left the area.","options":[],"answer":"steady","explanation":"选 M. steady（对应第 32 空）。steady work 意为‘稳定的工作’，正因工作稳定，工匠才不必离开此地。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"33. Critics still ____ that such projects mainly attract wealthy newcomers and push poorer residents out.","options":[],"answer":"argue","explanation":"选 B. argue（对应第 33 空）。argue that 表示‘认为、主张’，此处引出批评者的观点。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"34. That risk is real, and it must be ____ seriously.","options":[],"answer":"handled","explanation":"选 J. handled（对应第 34 空）。be handled seriously 意为‘被认真对待、处理’，情态动词 must 后接过去分词构成被动。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"blank","stem":"35. The answer is to plan carefully and accept that a city without memory is ____ to lose its identity.","options":[],"answer":"likely","explanation":"选 K. likely（对应第 35 空）。be likely to do 意为‘很可能……’，作者借此强调保护城市记忆的重要性。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"Some museums hold special mornings so that young children may touch objects and walk shorter distances.","options":["Telling stories rather than listing dates","Special mornings for young children","Room for study and work","Money remains a problem","Inviting residents to decide","The risk of pleasing everyone"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B 说 many museums run special mornings when the lights are brighter, the paths are shorter and touching is allowed，与题干对应，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"Curators now hope visitors will remember a story instead of a series of facts.","options":["Telling stories rather than listing dates","Special mornings for young children","Room for study and work","Money remains a problem","Inviting residents to decide","The risk of pleasing everyone"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A 说 they want people to leave with a story rather than a list of dates，与题干同义，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"Some museums let students use their libraries and offer quiet rooms during exam seasons.","options":["Telling stories rather than listing dates","Special mornings for young children","Room for study and work","Money remains a problem","Inviting residents to decide","The risk of pleasing everyone"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C 提到允许学生晚间使用图书馆，并说 offers a quiet study room to local teenagers during the exam season，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"Falling public funding and rising heating costs have led some museums to charge for entry.","options":["Telling stories rather than listing dates","Special mornings for young children","Room for study and work","Money remains a problem","Inviting residents to decide","The risk of pleasing everyone"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D 说 Government grants have fallen... the cost of heating... keeps rising，随后 Some museums have introduced ticket prices，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"In one town, residents chose to give the largest room to the history of local industry.","options":["Telling stories rather than listing dates","Special mornings for young children","Room for study and work","Money remains a problem","Inviting residents to decide","The risk of pleasing everyone"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph E 说 In one town, a public meeting decided that the museum's largest room should be given to the history of local factories，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What was the three-dimensional model of Notre-Dame used for?","options":["To guide the rebuilding after the fire.","To attract more tourists to the cathedral.","To replace the damaged stonework.","To train young architects in Paris."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说 The model did not prevent the fire, but it made the rebuilding far more accurate，即模型用于指导重建，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) How are the high-resolution images of the Lascaux cave paintings used?","options":["To sell copies to foreign museums.","To replace the paintings inside the caves.","For both research and education.","To keep visitors away from the caves."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说 the images are now used for both research and education，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why are digital images especially useful for fragile objects?","options":["They are cheaper than a museum ticket.","They can be studied without putting the objects at risk.","They are always more beautiful than the originals.","They can be printed and sold as souvenirs."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段说 They allow people to see objects that are too fragile to be displayed，并举对光敏感的十世纪手稿为例，说明数字图像可让人研究而不损伤原件，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do critics of digitisation worry about?","options":["The high cost of training engineers.","Too many visitors to small towns.","The loss of jobs for museum guides.","Money being spent on scans instead of repairs."],"answer":"D","explanation":"最后一段说 Screens may tempt governments to pay for images rather than for repairs, since a scan is cheaper than a new roof，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) Why did few young people want to learn book repair in the past?","options":["The work was too physically demanding.","The training programme was too short.","The pay was low and progress was invisible.","The materials were impossible to find."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说 the pay was low, the work was slow, and success was invisible，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What brought hundreds of new applicants to the training programme?","options":["A documentary that became popular online.","A large rise in the salary of restorers.","A new law protecting old books.","An article in a school textbook."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说 The turning point came when a documentary about restorers became unexpectedly popular online，随后报名人数大增，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-04","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) How does Zhou Lan view the sudden public interest in her trade?","options":["She thinks short films describe the work accurately.","She regards it as a useful starting point.","She believes it wastes the restorers' time.","She feels that payment should not be discussed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"最后一段说 she thinks the interest is a beginning，并说 People come for the pictures... Some of them stay for the paper，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"26. Students who spend a few hours a week at a community centre often say they gain as much as they ____.","options":[],"answer":"give","explanation":"选 D. give（对应第 26 空）。gain as much as they give 意为‘收获与付出一样多’，与前半句 benefits do not flow in one direction only 呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"27. The first weeks, though, can feel ____: a new place, unfamiliar faces and tasks that look simple but are not.","options":[],"answer":"awkward","explanation":"选 A. awkward（对应第 27 空）。冒号后列举‘陌生的地方、陌生的面孔’，说明最初几周会感到别扭、不自在。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"28. What makes the difference is ____ — turning up every week rather than once, when nothing else is planned.","options":[],"answer":"consistency","explanation":"选 B. consistency（对应第 28 空）。破折号后的解释‘每周都来，而不是没别的事才来一次’正是 consistency（坚持一致）的含义。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"29. Organisers say the most ____ volunteers are not always the most talented ones, but those who keep their promises.","options":[],"answer":"reliable","explanation":"选 J. reliable（对应第 29 空）。but those who keep their promises 说明组织者看重的是‘可靠’，与 talented 形成对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"30. Volunteering also teaches skills that are hard to ____ in a classroom, such as listening patiently and explaining an idea in simple words.","options":[],"answer":"learn","explanation":"选 F. learn（对应第 30 空）。‘在课堂上难以学到的技能’与后文列举的倾听、解释能力相配，learn 与 skills 构成动宾搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"31. Employers have begun to ____ such experience.","options":[],"answer":"recognise","explanation":"选 I. recognise（对应第 31 空）。recognise 在此意为‘认可、重视’，表示雇主开始看重这类经历。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"32. Some universities now ____ volunteer work in the record students take to interviews.","options":[],"answer":"include","explanation":"选 E. include（对应第 32 空）。include A in B 意为‘把 A 列入 B’，即把志愿工作写入求职材料。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"33. Volunteering should not ____ the place of paid work, nor should it damage a student's studies.","options":[],"answer":"take","explanation":"选 L. take（对应第 33 空）。take the place of 意为‘取代’，本句表示志愿服务不应取代有报酬的工作。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"34. What ____ is a modest but steady commitment.","options":[],"answer":"matters","explanation":"选 G. matters（对应第 34 空）。What matters is... 意为‘重要的是……’，总结全文对志愿服务的态度。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"blank","stem":"35. The habit, once ____, often lasts for life.","options":[],"answer":"formed","explanation":"选 C. formed（对应第 35 空）。once formed 为‘连词 + 过去分词’结构，表示习惯一旦养成便会持续一生。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"The garden started when residents turned an empty piece of land into something productive.","options":["From waste ground to a waiting list","Sharing the work through a rota","Children learning where food comes from","Sharing the harvest with the neighbourhood","Settling disagreements in a fixed way","A future that depends on the land"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A 讲一片杂草丛生的空地如何被居民开垦成菜园，最后还有人排队等候加入，与题干对应，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"Members take turns to look after shared jobs, and the rota teaches newcomers how much work is involved.","options":["From waste ground to a waiting list","Sharing the work through a rota","Children learning where food comes from","Sharing the harvest with the neighbourhood","Settling disagreements in a fixed way","A future that depends on the land"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B 说 a rota covers the weekends，并提到 rota matters more than the rules on paper, because it shows new members how much work one tomato plant demands，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"School classes visit the garden so that pupils can see how vegetables are grown.","options":["From waste ground to a waiting list","Sharing the work through a rota","Children learning where food comes from","Sharing the harvest with the neighbourhood","Settling disagreements in a fixed way","A future that depends on the land"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C 说 Nearby primary schools treat the garden as an open-air classroom，孩子们 first time they have seen food growing in soil，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"Part of the produce is given to older neighbours and to a food bank.","options":["From waste ground to a waiting list","Sharing the work through a rota","Children learning where food comes from","Sharing the harvest with the neighbourhood","Settling disagreements in a fixed way","A future that depends on the land"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D 说 Some of it reaches elderly neighbours... and some goes to a food bank two streets away，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"It is still not certain that the garden can stay on the land in the long term.","options":["From waste ground to a waiting list","Sharing the work through a rota","Children learning where food comes from","Sharing the harvest with the neighbourhood","Settling disagreements in a fixed way","A future that depends on the land"],"answer":"F","explanation":"Paragraph F 说 the land remains uncertain, The council could sell the site for housing，因此才要写入社区规划，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What are some careers advisers more interested in?","options":["What students gain from the community.","How much money volunteering raises.","Why charities refuse student helpers.","Which subjects volunteers should study."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说 careers advisers are more interested in the opposite question: what the community does for students，即关注学生从社区得到什么，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the study find about the improvement in students?","options":["It only appeared in students of medicine.","It disappeared when the volunteering stopped.","It did not depend on the type of work done.","It was greater for those who worked longer hours."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说 The improvement did not depend on the sort of work they did，并举例说整理旧衣物与教孩子游泳效果相似，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the survey of graduate recruiters show?","options":["Most recruiters ignore volunteering completely.","Recruiters prefer students with high grades.","Recruiters doubt whether volunteering is real work.","Over half of them linked volunteering with qualities such as reliability."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第三段说 more than half said that volunteering was useful evidence of qualities... such as reliability，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do advisers warn students against?","options":["Choosing more than one organisation.","Letting volunteering push aside sleep and study.","Working with children without training.","Taking part in activities that last a year."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第四段说 They also warn against letting hours push aside sleep, study or paid work，故选 B；A 恰恰相反，顾问建议只选一家机构。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What surprised the organisers about the replies to the questionnaire?","options":["Almost every household returned a form.","Very few residents offered any skills.","The most common request was for company.","Residents asked for an app instead of a booklet."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说 Only about a fifth of the forms came back，与 A 相反；但 the most frequent request was not practical at all: many people asked for company，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) Why did the organisers print a booklet instead of building an app?","options":["An app would have cost too much to develop.","The local shop offered to pay for the printing.","A booklet could be updated more easily.","Older residents said screens made them feel excluded."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第三段说 Older residents told them that a screen was exactly the thing that had made them feel excluded，因此改印小册子，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-05","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) How do the organisers judge whether the project is working?","options":["By how quickly a new family is invited to something.","By the number of services exchanged each month.","By the number of booklets printed.","By how much money the project raises."],"answer":"A","explanation":"最后一段说 The organisers measure success not by the number of services exchanged but by how quickly newcomers are noticed，并以 a new family is invited to something within a month 作具体说明，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"26. A gesture that is friendly in one country can cause ____ in another.","options":[],"answer":"offence","explanation":"选 H. offence（对应第 26 空）。cause offence 意为‘冒犯他人’，与前半句 friendly 形成对比，故用名词 offence。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"27. A distance that feels natural may make another person ____.","options":[],"answer":"uncomfortable","explanation":"选 N. uncomfortable（对应第 27 空）。make sb + 形容词表示‘使某人……’，此处指你觉得自然的距离会让对方不舒服。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"28. This does not mean that body language is a list of rules to be learned ____.","options":[],"answer":"overnight","explanation":"选 I. overnight（对应第 28 空）。learned overnight 意为‘一夜之间学会’，与后文‘人们是无意识地掌握它的’形成对照。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"29. If a visitor keeps ____ his hands and shifting his feet, we may decide that he is nervous.","options":[],"answer":"rubbing","explanation":"选 K. rubbing（对应第 29 空）。keep doing sth 后接动名词，rub one's hands（搓手）与 shifting his feet 并列，均为紧张的外在表现。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"30. We may decide, without any ____, that he is nervous or dishonest.","options":[],"answer":"evidence","explanation":"选 E. evidence（对应第 30 空）。without any evidence 意为‘没有任何证据’，说明这种判断只是主观印象。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"31. The ____ advice is therefore to slow down.","options":[],"answer":"simplest","explanation":"选 L. simplest（对应第 31 空）。下文给出的都是‘放慢速度’一类朴素做法，故用形容词最高级 simplest 修饰 advice。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"32. Ask a colleague whether a habit that seems natural to you is ____ in the new setting.","options":[],"answer":"acceptable","explanation":"选 A. acceptable（对应第 32 空）。be acceptable in a setting 意为‘在某种场合可以被接受’，符合‘向同事确认习惯是否得体’之意。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"33. Above all, do not ____ politeness with agreement.","options":[],"answer":"confuse","explanation":"选 C. confuse（对应第 33 空）。confuse A with B 意为‘把 A 与 B 混为一谈’，冒号后的解释说明礼貌并不等于同意。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"34. None of this is ____.","options":[],"answer":"easy","explanation":"选 D. easy（对应第 34 空）。后文说读错信号是人人都会犯的错，说明这并不容易，用 easy 与前面‘放慢速度’的建议相呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"blank","stem":"35. The ability to laugh at one's own ____ is itself a form of cultural skill.","options":[],"answer":"mistakes","explanation":"选 F. mistakes（对应第 35 空）。laugh at one's own mistakes 意为‘笑对自己的错误’，复数与 one's own 搭配，也与前文 a mistake everyone makes 对应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"Cooks stand beside their dishes and explain the ingredients, so visitors can take recipes home.","options":["How the festival began","Food at the centre of the event","A stage shared by amateurs and professionals","The work done by volunteers","Finding the money to continue","A debate about growing bigger"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B 说 Cooks are encouraged to stand beside their dishes and explain what is in them, so that visitors leave with a recipe，与题干对应，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"The festival began when two neighbours decided to bring foreign families and older residents together.","options":["How the festival began","Food at the centre of the event","A stage shared by amateurs and professionals","The work done by volunteers","Finding the money to continue","A debate about growing bigger"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A 说 It started with two neighbours and a borrowed table，并说明目的是让外国邻居与老住户相互认识，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"The evening programme is given to ordinary residents, including a school choir and a student group.","options":["How the festival began","Food at the centre of the event","A stage shared by amateurs and professionals","The work done by volunteers","Finding the money to continue","A debate about growing bigger"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C 说 the evening programme belongs to amateurs: a primary school choir, a group of students learning flamenco，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"New helpers receive a short introduction and work alongside someone with experience.","options":["How the festival began","Food at the centre of the event","A stage shared by amateurs and professionals","The work done by volunteers","Finding the money to continue","A debate about growing bigger"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D 说 New volunteers attend a short briefing a week in advance, and each is paired with someone who worked the previous year，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"After a storm left the event in debt, the organisers turned to local businesses for support.","options":["How the festival began","Food at the centre of the event","A stage shared by amateurs and professionals","The work done by volunteers","Finding the money to continue","A debate about growing bigger"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph E 说 a sudden storm destroyed half the tents, and the festival ended with a debt，此后 organisers have asked local businesses for support，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What left the Chinese student in Germany exhausted?","options":["Making a great many small decisions every day.","Attending classes that were longer than before.","Cooking for her flatmates every evening.","Travelling to the city centre by bus."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说 Her energy was going into small decisions，并列举问候邻居、等公交等琐事，故 A 正确；文中明确说课时并不比国内长，排除 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do researchers say about cultural fatigue?","options":["It only affects students who are homesick.","It can affect students who are doing well in their studies.","It usually disappears within the first month.","It shows that the student chose the wrong country."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段说 It appears in students who are doing well academically as well as in those who are homesick，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why is the usual advice thought to be incomplete?","options":["It costs money that students do not have.","It is too difficult for most students to follow.","It describes the cure without naming the illness.","It only works for outgoing students."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段说 it describes the cure without naming the illness，即只给对策却没有指出问题所在，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What seems to help students feel more settled?","options":["Trying to be social every night.","Learning the language perfectly.","Living with a local family.","Keeping one or two fixed routines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第四段说 Students who keep one or two fixed habits... report feeling more settled than those who try to be social every night，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does the passage say about the word 'sorry'?","options":["It is no longer used in everyday English.","Real speakers use it for several different purposes.","It is the hardest word for foreign learners.","It always expresses sincere regret."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段列举了道歉、请人重复、打断会议、表示同情等多种用法，说明实际使用中它承担多种功能，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What made the difference between the two classes in the experiment?","options":["One class had more experienced grammar teachers.","One class studied twice as many new words.","One class watched recordings and discussed the choice of phrases.","One class spent a term studying abroad."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段说 one class also watched short recordings of everyday situations and discussed why the speaker chose a particular phrase，该班在角色扮演中表现更好，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the author's conclusion?","options":["Grammar tests should be abolished.","Vocabulary is less important than it used to be.","Students should read restaurant menus in English.","Language and culture should be learned together."],"answer":"D","explanation":"最后一段说 a language is not only a system of rules but also a set of habits used by a community，并以看菜单作比，说明语言与文化应一起学，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"26. Many adults believe that learning to play a musical instrument is a task for children, yet a growing body of research ____ that the adult brain is far more adaptable than was once thought.","options":[],"answer":"suggests","explanation":"选 O. suggests。research 为不可数名词作主语，谓语用第三人称单数，且后接 that 从句表示研究结果显示；indicate 形式不符，故排除。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"27. Teachers of adult beginners report that their students are often more ____ than teenagers, because they choose to be there.","options":[],"answer":"motivated","explanation":"选 H. motivated。be more motivated than 表示比青少年更有动力，与后半句 because they choose to be there 相呼应；permanent 不能修饰人的学习状态。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"28. The main ____ for adults is time.","options":[],"answer":"obstacle","explanation":"选 I. obstacle。the main obstacle 意为主要障碍，后文说工作与家庭占去练习时间，正是成年人学琴的主要困难；roughly 是副词，不能作主语。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"29. Teachers therefore ____ short, frequent sessions rather than one long one at the weekend.","options":[],"answer":"recommend","explanation":"选 K. recommend。recommend A rather than B 意为建议选 A 而不选 B，符合教师给成年初学者的练习建议；delay 与句意相反。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"30. Fifteen minutes a day is enough to ____ a new habit.","options":[],"answer":"establish","explanation":"选 E. establish。establish a habit 是固定搭配，意为养成新习惯；achieve 通常接 goal 或 success，不与 habit 搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"31. Experienced teachers ____ this anxiety by choosing simple pieces at first and by celebrating small improvements.","options":[],"answer":"reduce","explanation":"选 L. reduce。reduce anxiety 意为减轻焦虑，与后文先选简单曲目、肯定小进步的做法对应；ignore 与教师主动处理的做法矛盾。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"32. Studies ____ that playing an instrument engages memory, attention and coordination at the same time.","options":[],"answer":"indicate","explanation":"选 G. indicate。Studies 为复数主语，谓语用动词原形 indicate，后接 that 从句引出研究结论；suggests 为第三人称单数，在此不搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"33. Some researchers ____ that this mental exercise may help keep the brain healthy in later life.","options":[],"answer":"argue","explanation":"选 B. argue。argue that 意为主张、论证，符合研究者提出观点的语境；delay 后不能接 that 从句表达观点。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"34. This mental exercise may help keep the brain healthy in later life, although the evidence is not yet ____.","options":[],"answer":"conclusive","explanation":"选 C. conclusive。not yet conclusive 意为尚无定论，用于说明前面观点仍缺乏充分证据；permanent 意为永久的，与 evidence 不搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"blank","stem":"35. What is clear is that adults who begin lessons rarely ____ the decision, even if they never perform in public.","options":[],"answer":"regret","explanation":"选 M. regret。rarely regret the decision 意为很少后悔这个决定，与 even if they never perform in public 的让步语气一致；ignore 与 decision 搭配不当。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"Shoppers increasingly want to know where their food comes from and how it was produced.","options":["Growing demand","A shorter journey","Bringing people together","Challenges for small farmers","Changing habits of young people","What the future may hold"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段说 shoppers ask more questions about food: where it was grown, when it was picked and how it was transported，与题干「想知道食物来源与生产方式」对应，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"Markets give local residents a place to meet and talk, not just to shop.","options":["Growing demand","A shorter journey","Bringing people together","Challenges for small farmers","Changing habits of young people","What the future may hold"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段提到 neighbours meet while queuing for bread，并说市场已成为社区生活的中心，说明它不只是购物场所，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"Younger customers are trying unfamiliar vegetables and changing how they plan meals.","options":["Growing demand","A shorter journey","Bringing people together","Challenges for small farmers","Changing habits of young people","What the future may hold"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E 段指出年轻顾客会买下从未做过的蔬菜，短视频走红后这些菜成为畅销品，并说这种尝试正改变年轻人的备餐方式，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"Selling locally means the food is fresher and needs less transport and storage.","options":["Growing demand","A shorter journey","Bringing people together","Challenges for small farmers","Changing habits of young people","What the future may hold"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段说市场食物只运输几英里，可更接近成熟时采摘，也不需要长途运输所需的冷藏，与题干完全对应，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"Organisers believe markets will keep growing as people seek sustainable food.","options":["Growing demand","A shorter journey","Bringing people together","Challenges for small farmers","Changing habits of young people","What the future may hold"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F 段开头说参与者普遍乐观，As food prices rise and concern about the environment grows, buying directly from producers looks attractive，即需求会继续增长，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the survey find mattered most about an internship?","options":["How long it lasted.","The reputation of the company.","The salary that was paid.","The quality of feedback the student received."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Passage 1 第一段末尾明确说 the length of the internship mattered less than the quality of the feedback the students received，即反馈质量最重要，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why might a small studio offer a better internship than a large firm?","options":["It usually pays more money.","The manager explains every decision.","The work is much easier.","It lasts much longer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段举例说，在小工作室实习的学生有 the manager explained every decision，因而在面试中有更多可谈的内容，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What kind of tasks does the author value most?","options":["Tasks that require judgement.","Repetitive paperwork.","Tasks done straight from a textbook.","Photocopying documents."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第三段指出 Tasks that require judgement ... teach skills that cannot be learned from a textbook，而重复性工作虽好看却无话可谈，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why are unpaid internships often criticised?","options":["They last too long.","They offer no training at all.","They favour students from wealthier families.","They are against the law."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第四段说 Unpaid internships are often criticised because they favour students whose families can support them，即对家庭条件好的学生更有利，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does Li Na like about her work?","options":["It pays more than her office job.","It offers sick pay.","She has a manager to guide her.","She chooses her own hours."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Passage 2 开头说 She chooses her own hours，并引用她的话 I do not have a manager watching me，说明她喜欢自己安排时间，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is one advantage of gig work mentioned in the passage?","options":["It fits around other responsibilities.","It guarantees a monthly income.","It provides a pension.","It offers paid holidays."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说学生可以在课间挣钱、父母可兼顾照看孩子，For people who find fixed schedules difficult, this flexibility is genuinely valuable，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-07","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the main concern about gig workers?","options":["They earn too much money.","They work too few hours.","They lack basic protections.","They are unable to use apps."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段列举他们通常没有带薪假期、病假和雇主缴纳的养老金，且平台改规则时难以协商，核心问题是缺乏基本保障，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"26. The front of a packet is designed to ____ your attention.","options":[],"answer":"attract","explanation":"选 A. attract。attract attention 意为吸引注意力，与主语 the front of a packet 搭配；maintain 意为维持，无法与 attention 构成吸引之意。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"27. Words such as 'natural' or 'light' sound healthy, but they have no ____ legal meaning in many countries.","options":[],"answer":"precise","explanation":"选 J. precise。no precise legal meaning 意为没有明确的法律含义，修饰 legal meaning 最贴切；generous 意为慷慨的，与此处语义无关。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"28. Check the size of the portion the numbers ____ to.","options":[],"answer":"refer","explanation":"选 K. refer。refer to 意为指的是，此处说明包装上的数字对应的是哪一份的量；decline 意为下降，不能与 to 构成此义。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"29. Compare the same kind of product rather than two different foods, because ____ values are easier to judge.","options":[],"answer":"similar","explanation":"选 M. similar。similar values 意为相近的数值，同类产品的营养成分更容易比较；frequently 是副词，不能修饰名词 values。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"30. Nutritionists advise shoppers to ____ products with a long list of unfamiliar ingredients.","options":[],"answer":"avoid","explanation":"选 B. avoid。avoid products 意为避开这类产品，与前文「成分表越短加工越少」的观点一致；maintain products 搭配不当。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"31. They also ____ that sugar appears under many names.","options":[],"answer":"warn","explanation":"选 N. warn。warn that 意为提醒、警告，营养学家提醒消费者糖会以多种名称出现；decline 后接 that 从句表示拒绝，语义不符。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"32. A product may contain three kinds of sweetener and still look ____ at first glance.","options":[],"answer":"harmless","explanation":"选 H. harmless。look harmless 意为看起来无害，与前面暗中含多种甜味剂形成对比；generous 不能描述食品是否无害。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"33. Food companies argue that labels give consumers ____ to make their own choices.","options":[],"answer":"freedom","explanation":"选 F. freedom。give sb freedom to do sth 意为给予某人做某事的自由，符合食品公司为标签辩护的立场；budget 指预算，与选择权无关。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"34. Campaigners agree in principle but want the ____ to be clearer.","options":[],"answer":"wording","explanation":"选 O. wording。the wording 指标签上的措辞，与 clearer 搭配自然；budget 与措辞是否清晰无关。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"blank","stem":"35. Shoppers can protect themselves with one simple ____: turn the packet over and read the small print.","options":[],"answer":"rule","explanation":"选 L. rule。one simple rule 意为一条简单的规则，冒号后正是这条规则的内容；maintain 是动词，不能作介词 with 的宾语。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"Independent shops and cafes have appeared where customers pay to play games together.","options":["Time away from screens","Old games, new players","A boost for small shops","Learning to lose","Barriers to growth","The table as a meeting place"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段介绍许多城市开设桌游咖啡馆，顾客付少量费用借用游戏，并说这是小店主与网店竞争的方式，与题干对应，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"Many families choose games as a way to spend an evening without screens.","options":["Time away from screens","Old games, new players","A boost for small shops","Learning to lose","Barriers to growth","The table as a meeting place"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段说四成家庭每周至少一起玩游戏一次，多数人表示主要原因是 to spend an evening without phones，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"Playing games teaches children to accept defeat and wait their turn.","options":["Time away from screens","Old games, new players","A boost for small shops","Learning to lose","Barriers to growth","The table as a meeting place"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段列举 Waiting for your turn ... and accepting defeat are all practised at the table，并说某小学因此减少了操场争执，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"Game nights give newcomers and neighbours a chance to meet and make friends.","options":["Time away from screens","Old games, new players","A boost for small shops","Learning to lose","Barriers to growth","The table as a meeting place"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F 段说对搬到新城市的人而言，游戏之夜是认识人的便捷方式，常客常常独自前来、结伴离开，与题干对应，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"Classic games are attracting interest from a younger generation.","options":["Time away from screens","Old games, new players","A boost for small shops","Learning to lose","Barriers to growth","The table as a meeting place"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段说祖辈玩过的经典游戏重新上架，购买者常是二三十岁的年轻人，即经典游戏吸引了年轻一代，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do many students do when exams approach?","options":["They cut back on sleep.","They stop revising altogether.","They sleep more than usual.","They change their diet."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passage 1 开头说 Students often treat sleep as the first thing to cut when exams approach，即考试临近时学生最先削减睡眠，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What happens during deep sleep according to the passage?","options":["The brain rests completely.","New words are forgotten.","Students become distracted.","Memories are reorganised and linked."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段说 the brain replays and reorganises what it has taken in, linking new material to what we already know，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the experiment with the word list show?","options":["Revising all night is effective.","Students need less sleep than adults.","Those who slept remembered more.","All students remembered the same amount."],"answer":"C","explanation":"实验发现学完单词后睡八小时的学生，第二天记住的内容远多于同样时长保持清醒复习的学生，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the author suggest for a forced late night?","options":["Drinking more coffee.","Taking a short nap the next day.","Sleeping late at weekends.","Skipping breakfast."],"answer":"B","explanation":"末段建议 try to recover with a short nap the next day rather than with more coffee，即第二天小睡补觉，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the resolution curve?","options":["A kind of gym equipment.","A twelve-week training programme.","A method of measuring fitness.","The pattern of new members dropping out."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Passage 2 开头说健身房一月人满为患，三月许多人悄然消失，staff call it the resolution curve，即新会员退出的规律，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What made the second group in the study more successful?","options":["They had more free time.","They linked exercise to an existing routine.","They exercised alone.","They chose expensive gyms."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二组被要求把锻炼与既有日常安排联系起来，如早上听完课直接去健身房，出勤次数约为第一组的两倍，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-08","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does the passage say about starting small?","options":["It makes people more likely to keep exercising.","It wastes the first month.","It is suitable only for children.","It requires a personal trainer."],"answer":"A","explanation":"研究发现从每天十分钟开始的人一个月后仍在锻炼的比例更高，因为十分钟很容易做到，不会让人想跳过，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"26. Typing has become the ____ way for students to take notes.","options":[],"answer":"standard","explanation":"选 K. standard。the standard way 意为常规方式，说明打字已成为学生记笔记的主要方式；remote 意为偏远的，不能修饰 way。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"27. Teachers report that most essays are now ____ on a screen.","options":[],"answer":"composed","explanation":"选 B. composed。compose an essay 意为撰写文章，此处用被动语态表示论文在屏幕上完成；maintain 指维护，与 essay 搭配不当。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"28. A page of cursive writing looks almost ____ to some teenagers.","options":[],"answer":"unfamiliar","explanation":"选 M. unfamiliar。look unfamiliar to sb 意为对某人来说很陌生，与许多青少年几乎读不懂自己字迹的描述一致；familiar 意思相反。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"29. Yet handwriting may still have something to ____.","options":[],"answer":"offer","explanation":"选 H. offer。have something to offer 意为仍有可取之处，引出下文手写对记忆的好处；maintain 不能用于该固定结构。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"30. Researchers find a small but consistent ____: students who write notes by hand tend to remember more.","options":[],"answer":"advantage","explanation":"选 A. advantage。a consistent advantage 意为持续的优势，冒号后正是这项优势的具体内容；warning 意为警告，与语境不符。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"31. Students who write notes by hand must ____ information and choose what matters.","options":[],"answer":"summarise","explanation":"选 L. summarise。summarise information 意为概括信息，与后面 choose what matters 对应；recall 指回忆，不能体现记笔记时的处理过程。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"32. Handwriting is also ____ to memory in another way.","options":[],"answer":"linked","explanation":"选 F. linked。be linked to 意为与……相关联，说明手写与记忆还有另一层联系；familiar 不能与 to memory 构成此义。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"33. The physical movement of the pen leaves a trace that the brain can later ____.","options":[],"answer":"recall","explanation":"选 I. recall。recall a trace 意为回忆起痕迹，说明书写动作留下的记忆线索日后可被提取；maintain 意为维持，与 trace 搭配不当。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"34. Few people ____ that keyboards are here to stay.","options":[],"answer":"doubt","explanation":"选 D. doubt。Few people doubt that 意为很少有人怀疑，即大家普遍认可键盘不会消失；devote 后不能接 that 从句表达怀疑。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"blank","stem":"35. The question is whether schools should ____ a little time each week to the old skill.","options":[],"answer":"devote","explanation":"选 C. devote。devote time to sth 意为把时间用于某事，符合是否应每周留出一点时间练习书写的句意；offer 不能与 time to 构成此搭配。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"Travelling by train produces far less carbon than flying the same distance.","options":["Slower journeys, richer experiences","Night trains return to the timetable","The cost of going slowly","A smaller footprint","Business travellers stay away","Planning a route"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段指出按每位乘客计算，欧洲境内火车产生的二氧化碳只是同程飞机的一小部分，与题干对应，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"Several countries are bringing back overnight services that had been cancelled.","options":["Slower journeys, richer experiences","Night trains return to the timetable","The cost of going slowly","A smaller footprint","Business travellers stay away","Planning a route"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段说夜间列车在多年衰退后重新出现在欧洲时刻表上，新线路连接维也纳、巴黎等城市，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"Travellers need to check connections and timetables carefully before setting off.","options":["Slower journeys, richer experiences","Night trains return to the timetable","The cost of going slowly","A smaller footprint","Business travellers stay away","Planning a route"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F 段强调慢旅行更依赖准备：Timetables must be checked, because a missed connection can mean a whole day lost，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"A slow journey lets travellers see places that planes and motorways pass by.","options":["Slower journeys, richer experiences","Night trains return to the timetable","The cost of going slowly","A smaller footprint","Business travellers stay away","Planning a route"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段说从车窗能看到坐飞机时消失的村庄、河流与田野，还可以中途停留，说明慢行让人看见被略过的地方，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"Slow travel is not always cheaper, because extra nights add to the expense.","options":["Slower journeys, richer experiences","Night trains return to the timetable","The cost of going slowly","A smaller footprint","Business travellers stay away","Planning a route"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段说晚订的火车票可能比廉价机票更贵，每多住一晚都会增加总花费，与题干一致，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) How did city planners measure success in the past?","options":["By the number of pedestrians.","By the amount of green space.","By the speed of car traffic.","By the price of housing."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Passage 1 开头说 planners measured success by how quickly cars could move，即以汽车通行速度衡量成功，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why did shops on pedestrian streets sell more?","options":["Walkers stop and look at the windows.","Cars could park right outside.","Rents on those streets were lower.","The streets were much longer."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说 shops on busy pedestrian streets sold more, because walkers stop and look, while drivers pass by，即行人会停下观看，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Which measure is described as simple and cheap?","options":["Building a new underground railway.","A decade of road construction.","Removing all the street trees.","Lower speed limits in residential areas."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第三段列举的简单低成本措施包括 narrower lanes, raised crossings, more street trees and lower speed limits in residential areas，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What problem can pedestrian-friendly schemes cause?","options":["More serious accidents.","Rising rents for local businesses.","Much colder winters.","Fewer shops in the city centre."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第四段指出这类方案 can raise rents and push out the small businesses they were meant to help，即租金上涨可能挤走本要帮助的小商户，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What do planners often fail to consider about a new square?","options":["The cost of the paving.","The number of trees to plant.","Whether people will actually use it.","How it will look in photographs."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Passage 2 开头说照片拍不出的是 whether anyone will use the space，并指出漂亮的公共空间可能空无一人，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) Why do successful public spaces offer several kinds of activity?","options":["To earn more money from rent.","So that different groups feel welcome and safe.","To keep the space quiet and empty.","To save money on benches."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段说只被某一群体使用的空间会让其他人感到不安全，多样活动能让儿童、老人等不同人群都愿意停留，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-09","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does Janet Byrne say about the value of public spaces?","options":["It can be fully measured in money.","It is mainly good for tourism.","It matters only for young children.","It teaches people how to live together."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Janet Byrne 说 A good public space teaches a city how to behave，并举例陌生人共用一张桌子、互相认识与信任，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(26) When people try to ____ into a new routine, the hardest part is usually the first three weeks.","options":[],"answer":"settle","explanation":"选 J. settle。settle into a new routine 意为「适应、安顿于新的日常安排」，与后文「最难的是头三周」呼应；force 与 into 搭配不当。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(27) Scientists who ____ the behaviour of two hundred volunteers found that a habit is built when an action is repeated in the same place at the same time.","options":[],"answer":"tracked","explanation":"选 L. tracked。track the behaviour of sb 意为「追踪某人的行为」，是本句研究方法的准确表达；maintain 意为「维持」，与「研究发现」的语境不符。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(28) In one study, students who put ____ on their desks to drink more water were far more successful than those who simply promised to try harder.","options":[],"answer":"reminders","explanation":"选 I. reminders。put reminders on their desks 意为「在桌上放提醒物」，与后文「只靠发誓要更努力」形成对照；此处需复数名词。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(29) In that study, students who made the new behaviour easy were far more ____ to succeed than those who simply promised to try harder.","options":[],"answer":"likely","explanation":"选 G. likely。be likely to do 意为「有可能做某事」，far more likely to succeed 表示「成功的可能性大得多」；effective 不能接 to do。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(30) The pattern was ____ in offices and in schools.","options":[],"answer":"similar","explanation":"选 K. similar。the pattern was similar in ... and ... 意为「这一规律在……和……中相似」，说明结论具有普遍性；unexpected 与上文一致的研究发现相矛盾。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(31) Researchers therefore advise people to increase the difficulty only ____.","options":[],"answer":"gradually","explanation":"选 F. gradually。仅用程度副词 gradually 与 increase 搭配，表示「只应逐步提高难度」；此处不能填形容词或名词。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(32) Researchers therefore advise people never to ____ themselves to change everything at once.","options":[],"answer":"force","explanation":"选 E. force。force oneself to do 意为「强迫自己去做」，never to force themselves 与句中 at once 形成劝阻语气。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(33) After a few months the new action becomes ____, and the effort disappears.","options":[],"answer":"automatic","explanation":"选 A. automatic。become automatic 意为「变得自然而然、自动发生」，与后半句 the effort disappears 对应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(34) The real ____ is not a perfect week but a routine you can (35) even when you are tired or busy.","options":[],"answer":"benefit","explanation":"选 B. benefit。空格前有 The real，需填名词；benefit 意为「益处」，说明真正的收获是可长期坚持的习惯，而非完美的一周。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"blank","stem":"(35) A routine that you can ____ even when you are tired or busy is worth far more than one perfect week.","options":[],"answer":"maintain","explanation":"选 H. maintain。maintain a routine 意为「维持一种日常习惯」，与从句 even when you are tired or busy 的让步语气一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"Managers use information about what is ordered to decide what to cook.","options":["Queues that vanished","Robots behind the counter","What students miss","Data on the menu","A cheaper way to run a canteen","What comes next"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段说 Managers can now see that the vegetarian dish is popular on Wednesdays... This has changed the menu，即管理者依据销售数据调整菜单，与题干对应，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"Some of the routine cooking in the kitchen is now done by machines.","options":["Queues that vanished","Robots behind the counter","What students miss","Data on the menu","A cheaper way to run a canteen","What comes next"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段介绍厨房里的两台机器，一台搅汤、一台煎蛋，并说 the machines handle the boring work，正是题干所说「部分常规烹饪由机器完成」，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"Some students feel that the personal side of mealtimes has been lost.","options":["Queues that vanished","Robots behind the counter","What students miss","Data on the menu","A cheaper way to run a canteen","What comes next"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段提到学生发起请愿，希望保留至少一个人工窗口，其理由 being treated as a person rather than a number，即怀念用餐时的人情味，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"Waiting in long lines is no longer part of the lunch break.","options":["Queues that vanished","Robots behind the counter","What students miss","Data on the menu","A cheaper way to run a canteen","What comes next"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段对比过去排队二十分钟与如今 the queue has almost disappeared，说明排队已基本消失，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"The next stage may involve menus designed around each student's health needs.","options":["Queues that vanished","Robots behind the counter","What students miss","Data on the menu","A cheaper way to run a canteen","What comes next"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F 段介绍正在试验 a menu that adjusts to each student's health data，并说明该试验先小范围自愿参与，与题干「下一阶段可能按个人健康需求设计菜单」对应，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the passage say about the number of service robots sold worldwide?","options":["It has doubled in ten years.","It has more than tripled in five years.","It fell sharply during the pandemic.","It has stayed the same for a decade."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Passage 1 首段说 the number of service robots sold worldwide has more than tripled in five years，即五年间增长了两倍多，故选 B；疫情期间是加快了趋势而非下降，C 错。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do robots do in Japanese hospitals, according to the passage?","options":["They carry out operations on patients.","They talk to patients about their illness.","They carry meals and laundry, saving nurses about two hours a day.","They check the work of doctors."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说 In hospitals in Japan, robots carry meals and laundry, which saves nurses perhaps two hours a day，故选 C；文中并未说机器人做手术或与病人讨论病情。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the survey of hotel guests find?","options":["Fewer than a third would trust a robot with a problem such as a lost booking.","Most guests refused to enter a hotel with robots.","Guests preferred robots to human staff in every situation.","Guests were willing to pay more for robot service."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第三段说虽然多数客人对机器人好奇，but fewer than a third said they would trust one with a problem such as a lost booking，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do researchers say about the limits of service robots?","options":["They are too expensive for hospitals to buy.","They make more mistakes than tired people do.","They need to be replaced every two years.","They work badly when the unexpected happens."],"answer":"D","explanation":"末段说 Robots work well when a task can be described step by step, and badly when the unexpected happens，并举小孩跑进走廊为例，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What has made the old model of learning a trade unreliable?","options":["Fewer young people want to work in factories.","Automation now does tasks that once filled a working day.","Companies have stopped offering training.","Trade schools have closed down."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Passage 2 首段说 Automation has made that model much less reliable，并说明机器已能处理归档、计算乃至部分写作，「曾占满工作日」的任务被机器接手，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) Which abilities did the survey find most often named in job advertisements?","options":["Programming and bookkeeping.","Driving and operating machines.","Clear communication, problem solving and quick learning.","Foreign languages and public speaking."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段列出招聘广告中最常提到的三项能力：communicate clearly, solve unfamiliar problems, learn something new quickly，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-mock-10","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What unexpected detail did the researchers note about the training programme?","options":["Older workers finished faster than young ones.","The courses were too expensive to continue.","Most participants left their jobs afterwards.","Workers who studied with a colleague were twice as likely to finish."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第三段说 the researchers noted one unexpected detail: workers who studied with a colleague were twice as likely to finish，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(26) Thirty years ago most parents could ____ that everyone would be at the table by seven.","options":[],"answer":"expect","explanation":"选 C. expect。expect that... 意为「预料到、指望」，与后半句如今「几乎不可能」形成时间对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(27) Today work, sport and screens make that ____ impossible.","options":[],"answer":"almost","explanation":"选 A. almost。almost impossible 意为「几乎不可能」，是常见搭配；complete 作定语时不能修饰 impossible，表示「完全不可能」须用副词 completely。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(28) Families that eat together at least four times a week ____ far more about each other's lives.","options":[],"answer":"learn","explanation":"选 G. learn。learn about each other's lives 意为「了解彼此的生活」，与前句「一起吃饭」构成因果。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(29) Children in such families are ____ to do well at school.","options":[],"answer":"likely","explanation":"选 H. likely。be likely to do 意为「有可能做某事」，此处指常一起吃饭家庭的孩子学业表现更好。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(30) Some busy households now keep one ____ dinner a week and treat it as an appointment that cannot be moved.","options":[],"answer":"fixed","explanation":"选 D. fixed。a fixed dinner 意为「固定的一顿晚餐」，与后文「当作不能改期的约定」呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(31) Others ____ the cooking among all members, so that nobody has to (32) a whole meal alone.","options":[],"answer":"share","explanation":"选 L. share。share sth among sb 意为「在……之间分摊」，与后半句「没有人需要独自做一整顿饭」一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(32) With the work divided among all members, nobody has to ____ a whole meal alone.","options":[],"answer":"prepare","explanation":"选 J. prepare。prepare a meal 意为「做一顿饭」，是固定搭配；空格后为 a whole meal，需要及物动词。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(33) Experts advise parents to keep the food plain and to let children ____ the conversation.","options":[],"answer":"join","explanation":"选 F. join。join the conversation 意为「加入交谈」，说明专家建议让孩子参与饭桌对话。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(34) A ____ dish cooked by a child may be the beginning of a lifelong habit.","options":[],"answer":"simple","explanation":"选 M. simple。a simple dish 意为「一道简单的菜」，与上句「食物保持简单」相承；whole 与 a dish 搭配不合逻辑。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"blank","stem":"(35) The point, they say, is not the food but the ____ of sitting down together.","options":[],"answer":"pleasure","explanation":"选 I. pleasure。the pleasure of doing sth 意为「做某事的乐趣」，与 not the food but... 的对比结构相符。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"Adults who take up an instrument often give up in the first few weeks.","options":["A late start, a real benefit","The first month is the hardest","Practice in small pieces","Finding the right teacher","Playing with others","What it costs"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段指出 where students give up, and the answer is almost always the same: the fourth or fifth week，即放弃多发生在第四五周，与题干一致，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"Research suggests that learning an instrument in later life can help the brain.","options":["A late start, a real benefit","The first month is the hardest","Practice in small pieces","Finding the right teacher","Playing with others","What it costs"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段提到中年开始学琴的成年人在记忆力和同时处理两条对话的能力上有提高，并说 the effort itself... keeps the brain busy，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"Beginners are advised to divide a piece into short sections instead of playing it from start to finish.","options":["A late start, a real benefit","The first month is the hardest","Practice in small pieces","Finding the right teacher","Playing with others","What it costs"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段首句即说 do not practise the whole piece，并建议 Divide it into sections of four or eight bars，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"Making music with other people gives practice a purpose and helps learners continue.","options":["A late start, a real benefit","The first month is the hardest","Practice in small pieces","Finding the right teacher","Playing with others","What it costs"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E 段说 the students who keep going are usually the ones who play with others，并提到合奏 gives the practice a purpose and a date，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"A good teacher should ask new students what they hope to achieve.","options":["A late start, a real benefit","The first month is the hardest","Practice in small pieces","Finding the right teacher","Playing with others","What it costs"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段建议先想清楚自己真正想要什么，并说 A good teacher asks about these goals in the first lesson，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What is said about audiobook sales?","options":["They have grown by double digits every year for a decade.","They fell as soon as streaming services appeared.","They are now larger than printed book sales.","They depend mainly on university libraries."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Passage 1 首段说 sales have risen by double digits every year for a decade，即连续十年保持两位数增长，故选 A；文中只说在某些市场每五本书有一本以有声形式阅读，并未说销量超过纸质书，C 过度推断。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why have some authors begun to write with narration in mind?","options":["They are paid more for audio editions.","They no longer write for libraries.","They want their books to be made into films.","They want their books to sound good when read aloud."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段说有些作者开始 with narration in mind, shortening long descriptions that sound slow when spoken，即为了让文字朗读出来效果好，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the study of university students find?","options":["Listeners understood the main idea worse than readers.","Listeners remembered the main idea but fewer details.","Students preferred audio to paper in every subject.","Most students gave up halfway through the chapter."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段说 those who only listened remembered fewer details of a chapter than those who read it on paper, although they remembered the main idea equally well，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What is the author's conclusion about the two forms of reading?","options":["They suit different kinds of books.","Paper books will disappear within a decade.","Listening should replace reading in schools.","Audio editions are cheaper to produce."],"answer":"A","explanation":"末段说 the two forms do different jobs，故事与访谈适合听，教科书与合同仍需用眼，即两种形式适合不同种类的书，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What happened to the cinema building after the cinema closed in 2011?","options":["It was turned into a pub.","It stood empty for ten years.","It was used for storing animal feed.","It became a school hall."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Passage 2 首段说 the building was sold and used for storing animal feed，故选 C；文中说此后四年小镇只有酒馆里的电视，并未说电影院变成了酒馆，A 为张冠李戴。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) How is the Rex run now?","options":["By a national cinema chain.","By a committee of twelve volunteers.","By the local government alone.","By the retired teacher on her own."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段说 It is run by a committee of twelve volunteers，故选 B；Mary 只是其中负责选片的一员，并非独自经营，D 不准确。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2023-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What difficulty is mentioned in the passage?","options":["Few films are available for the small screen.","Young people are unwilling to work there.","The town has decided to close the cinema again.","Membership has slipped as streaming services have grown."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第三段说 Membership has slipped slightly as streaming services have grown，即流媒体兴起使会员人数略有下降，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(26) In several cities a market building that had been left ____ for years now opens four days a week.","options":[],"answer":"vacant","explanation":"选 N. vacant。leave a building vacant 意为「使建筑空置」，与后文「如今每周开放四天」形成对比。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(27) A stall has low rents, so fruit and bread are often ____ than in a large shop.","options":[],"answer":"cheaper","explanation":"选 C. cheaper。空格后为 than，需用比较级；形容摊位租金低因而果蔬更便宜，cheaper 最贴切。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(28) A market can ____ vegetables from growers within thirty kilometres that supermarkets refuse to stock.","options":[],"answer":"offer","explanation":"选 H. offer。offer sth from growers 意为「提供来自种植者的某物」，主语是市场，与 supermarkets refuse to stock 形成对照。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(29) A market fills the space where shops have ____, and it turns a street into a place where people talk.","options":[],"answer":"closed","explanation":"选 D. closed。where shops have closed 意为「在商店已经关闭的地方」，说明集市填补了空铺面，用现在完成时的过去分词 closed。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(30) Some councils now offer a year of ____ rent for new traders, charged at half the usual rate.","options":[],"answer":"reduced","explanation":"选 K. reduced。reduced rent 意为「减免后的租金」，与后半句 charged at half the usual rate（按通常租金的一半收取）直接呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(31) Councils also give new traders ____ advice on rents, prices and paperwork.","options":[],"answer":"practical","explanation":"选 I. practical。practical advice 意为「实用的建议」，与减免租金同为地方政府扶持新摊主的措施。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(32) A market needs ____ management of stock, money and people, and one unsuccessful season can frighten traders away.","options":[],"answer":"careful","explanation":"选 B. careful。careful management of stock, money and people 意为「对货物、资金和人员的细致管理」，与后半句「一个失败的季节就会吓跑摊主」的风险形成因果；flexible 侧重「灵活」，无法体现对细节的谨慎。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(33) Where the plan works, the effect is ____: residents stop driving to the supermarket outside town.","options":[],"answer":"dramatic","explanation":"选 E. dramatic。the effect is dramatic 意为「效果显著」，冒号后的具体变化（居民不再开车去郊外超市）正是对这一判断的解释。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(34) Above all, a street that felt ____ begins to look alive.","options":[],"answer":"quiet","explanation":"选 J. quiet。felt quiet 与 look alive 构成反义对照，说明街道从冷清变得有生气；spacious 指空间宽敞，不合句意。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"blank","stem":"(35) People who had ____ met now greet each other by name.","options":[],"answer":"never","explanation":"选 G. never。had never met 意为「此前从未见过面」，与 now greet each other by name 形成前后转折。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"Recordings are popular, but many students watch them at high speed just before the exam.","options":["Lectures are not disappearing","The flipped classroom","Recording everything","A new kind of homework","What students still need","The cost of change"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段说 nearly everyone watched the recordings, most watched at double speed the night before the exam，与题干「考前高速刷录像」一致，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"In some courses students must study the material before they come to class.","options":["Lectures are not disappearing","The flipped classroom","Recording everything","A new kind of homework","What students still need","The cost of change"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段介绍翻转课堂：Students read a chapter or watch a short video before the class，课上时间用于讨论，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"Large lecture halls are still used and still have a purpose.","options":["Lectures are not disappearing","The flipped classroom","Recording everything","A new kind of homework","What students still need","The cost of change"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段开头说 Reports of the death of the lecture are premature，并说明大讲堂仍有不可替代的作用，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"Students still need personal attention that large lectures and videos cannot give.","options":["Lectures are not disappearing","The flipped classroom","Recording everything","A new kind of homework","What students still need","The cost of change"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E 段说学生需要有人注意到他们在挣扎、推荐书单、诚实评价论文，而 Large lectures cannot do this, and neither can videos，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"Redesigning a course takes a great deal of a teacher's time.","options":["Lectures are not disappearing","The flipped classroom","Recording everything","A new kind of homework","What students still need","The cost of change"],"answer":"F","explanation":"F 段说 Preparing a good video takes a teacher perhaps eight hours, and redesigning a course can take a whole summer，即改课耗时巨大，故选 F。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the British research suggest about a structured gap year?","options":["It is useful only for students who failed their school exams.","It is linked to slightly better results at university.","It makes students much more likely to drop out.","It costs more than a year of university study."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Passage 1 首段说学生 slightly less likely to drop out and slightly more likely to finish with a good degree，即与更好的学业结果略有相关，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why is it hard to separate the effect of the gap year from the person?","options":["Gap-year students rarely answer surveys.","Universities keep no records of gap years.","Gap-year activities are too different to compare.","Students who take a gap year are already more confident and organized."],"answer":"D","explanation":"首段末尾说 Students who take a gap year are already, on average, more confident and better organized than those who do not, so it is hard to separate the year from the person，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do young people gain from doing something difficult for a year?","options":["A guaranteed place on a prestigious course.","Higher marks in their final school exams.","Confidence and the knowledge that failure is survivable.","A career plan chosen by their parents."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说他们发现自己有用、大人会听他们说话，and that failure is survivable，即获得自信并明白失败可以承受，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What practical advice does the passage give to families?","options":["The year should have a plan, a purpose and someone to report back to.","The year should be spent as far from home as possible.","The year must be paid for by the university.","The year should be kept secret from friends."],"answer":"A","explanation":"末段建议 The year should have a plan, a purpose and someone to report back to，并说不必昂贵或远行，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What was the situation of the typical British high street ten years ago?","options":["Online shopping had not yet appeared.","Rents on the high street were falling fast.","One in eight shops stood empty.","Shop owners were already filming videos."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Passage 2 首段说 online shops were cheaper, took no rent and never closed, and one in eight high-street shops stood empty，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What made the hardware shop in Sheffield well known?","options":["Its very low prices.","Short videos of unusual repairs.","A television advertisement.","A large online delivery service."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段说 the owner began filming two-minute videos of unusual repairs，随后全国知名，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What do the successful shops have in common?","options":["They all employ a marketing agency.","They sell only products made locally.","They open seven days a week.","They post regularly in a voice that sounds personal."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段末尾说它们的共同点不是大预算而是习惯：someone posts two or three times a week, in a voice that sounds like a person，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"26. For many older people, online shopping has become a ______ way to get daily necessities.","options":[],"answer":"convenient","explanation":"此处需要形容词修饰 way，convenient 意为方便的，与后文不必提着重物回家相呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"27. Instead of carrying heavy bags home, they can place ______ on a mobile app.","options":[],"answer":"orders","explanation":"place orders 是固定搭配，意为下单；下文说商品被送到家门口，可见此处指订单。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"28. They can place orders on a mobile app and have them ______ to their door.","options":[],"answer":"delivered","explanation":"have sth. done 表示让某事被完成，delivered 与 to their door 搭配，指送货上门。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"29. However, not everyone finds the technology ______ at first.","options":[],"answer":"familiar","explanation":"find sth. + 形容词，familiar 表示熟悉的；however 提示这里说老年人起初并不熟悉这项技术。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"30. Some seniors ______ with small screens and passwords.","options":[],"answer":"struggle","explanation":"struggle with 意为在……方面感到吃力，与后面的小屏幕和密码搭配，说明老年人使用困难。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"31. Community centres have started free classes to help them overcome these ______.","options":[],"answer":"barriers","explanation":"overcome 后接 barriers（障碍），指前文提到的操作困难和对支付安全的担忧。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"32. Those who attend the classes ______ become more confident.","options":[],"answer":"gradually","explanation":"此处需要副词修饰 become，gradually 表示逐渐地，说明信心是慢慢建立起来的。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"33. Many say the service has made them feel less ______.","options":[],"answer":"isolated","explanation":"feel 后接形容词，isolated 意为孤单的；下文说快递员的敲门可能是全天唯一的交谈，说明服务减少了孤独感。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"34. Experts suggest that companies should make their apps simpler and offer more ______ support.","options":[],"answer":"reliable","explanation":"此处需要形容词修饰 support，reliable 意为可靠的，与 simpler 并列，都是对企业的建议。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"blank","stem":"35. This is especially important for older customers who are less ______ with digital tools.","options":[],"answer":"comfortable","explanation":"be comfortable with 表示对……感到自如，less comfortable 指老年用户对数字工具不太适应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a service that uses sensors to decide which bins need collecting?","options":["Street lights that save energy","Medicine at a distance","Bins that report their fill level","Rides for older travellers","Parks that cool themselves","Repair shops for broken things"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C 讲垃圾桶装上传感器、只在快满时才通知清运车，与题干中的 sensors 和 bins 对应，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a project that has reduced the electricity used for lighting?","options":["Street lights that save energy","Medicine at a distance","Bins that report their fill level","Rides for older travellers","Parks that cool themselves","Repair shops for broken things"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A 说路灯根据行人自动调暗或调亮，照明用电减少了近三分之一，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a transport service designed for people who find ordinary buses hard to use?","options":["Street lights that save energy","Medicine at a distance","Bins that report their fill level","Rides for older travellers","Parks that cool themselves","Repair shops for broken things"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D 提到老年居民觉得普通公交站远、台阶高、时刻表难读，于是有了可电话预约的小型电动接驳车，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a way for patients in remote areas to get advice from hospital doctors?","options":["Street lights that save energy","Medicine at a distance","Bins that report their fill level","Rides for older travellers","Parks that cool themselves","Repair shops for broken things"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B 描述偏远山镇的诊所通过视频设备让病人连线城市医院的医生，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a project that makes a public space cooler in summer?","options":["Street lights that save energy","Medicine at a distance","Bins that report their fill level","Rides for older travellers","Parks that cool themselves","Repair shops for broken things"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph E 说由停车场改造的小公园夏季温度比周边街道低约四度，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) Why have health authorities welcomed the use of smartwatches?","options":["They believe visible data encourages healthier habits.","They want to collect personal data for hospitals.","They think watches can replace family doctors.","They hope to sell more devices to young people."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说卫生部门欢迎这一趋势，理由是当人们能看到自己的数据时更可能锻炼、更少忽视警示信号，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the author say about the accuracy of wrist devices?","options":["It is better than that of hospital equipment.","It can be affected by movements and other factors.","It has improved greatly in recent months.","It is accurate enough for medical diagnosis."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段说腕表的心率读数会受运动干扰，睡眠数据靠推测，计步取决于佩戴方式，两款机型计步相差近 15%，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What problem do anxious users cause?","options":["They stop taking any exercise.","They damage their watches quickly.","They buy more devices than they need.","They add pressure on busy clinics."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段末说焦虑用户一天查看心率几十次，稍有变化就以为得了重病，于是去本就繁忙的诊所就诊，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What is the author's view of wearable devices?","options":["They should be banned in hospitals.","They are a waste of money for most people.","They are useful for spotting long-term patterns.","They will soon replace regular health checks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段说可穿戴设备并非无用，它们善于显示长期趋势，例如数周内活动量缓慢下降可以是给医生的有用信号，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What change does the author describe at the beginning of the passage?","options":["Bottled water has lost its status as the natural choice.","Bottled water has become more expensive than ever.","Tap water has disappeared from supermarket shelves.","Restaurants have stopped serving water to customers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"首段说二十年前瓶装水似乎是未来，如今城市安装公共直饮点、发放可重复使用的水瓶，说明它不再是默认选择，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What do supporters of refill stations argue?","options":["Bottled water contains too much sugar.","Refill stations should be run by the government.","Bottled water costs more and often tastes no better.","Reusable bottles are easier to carry than plastic ones."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说支持者指出自来水比瓶装水便宜得多，并质疑口感差异是否真有广告说的那么大，盲测中许多人分辨不出，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2024-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the main challenge facing the refill movement?","options":["Lack of support from city governments.","Making refill stations convenient for everyone.","The high cost of building the stations.","A shortage of clean water in big cities."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三、四段说真正的挑战是让直饮点几乎出现在人们去的所有地方，使接水比买水更方便，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"26. Every day, university canteens serve millions of meals, and a surprising ______ of food ends up in the bin.","options":[],"answer":"amount","explanation":"a surprising amount of 表示数量惊人的，用于不可数名词 food，说明食堂浪费量很大。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"27. A recent ______ at one university found that students waste about sixty grams of food per meal.","options":[],"answer":"survey","explanation":"此处需要名词作主语，survey 意为调查，与 found 搭配，指一项调查发现了这一数据。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"28. The same research found that students ______ away about sixty grams of food per meal on average.","options":[],"answer":"throw","explanation":"throw away 是固定搭配，意为扔掉，与 food 和 bin 呼应，指学生每餐平均扔掉约六十克食物。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"29. When the ______ offers only large dishes at a low price, people tend to take more than they can eat.","options":[],"answer":"menu","explanation":"此处需要名词作从句主语，menu 意为菜单；菜单上只有大份菜品且价格便宜，学生就容易取得过多。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"30. Busy students are often not ______ of how much they leave behind.","options":[],"answer":"aware","explanation":"be aware of 是固定搭配，意为意识到，说明忙碌的学生往往没注意自己剩下多少食物。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"31. Several universities are now trying to ______ the problem.","options":[],"answer":"reduce","explanation":"此处需要动词，reduce 意为减少，与 problem 搭配，指高校正努力减少浪费问题。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"32. Some canteens offer smaller ______ at a lower price.","options":[],"answer":"portions","explanation":"portion 意为一份饭菜，smaller portions 指分量更小的餐食，价格为 lower，符合语境。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"33. Others put up posters and ______ students to bring their remaining food home.","options":[],"answer":"encourage","explanation":"encourage sb. to do sth. 意为鼓励某人做某事，与 put up posters 并列，都是减少浪费的做法。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"34. Many students now take their ______ home rather than leaving them on the plate.","options":[],"answer":"leftovers","explanation":"leftovers 意为剩饭菜，take leftovers home 指把没吃完的饭菜带回家，符合食堂减少浪费的做法。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"blank","stem":"35. The canteens ask students to bring ______ boxes for the food they take away.","options":[],"answer":"reusable","explanation":"此处需要形容词修饰 boxes，reusable 意为可重复使用的，与减少垃圾的目标一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a change that helps passengers know exactly when their bus is coming?","options":["Cheaper tickets for everyone","Buses after midnight","Knowing when the bus will come","Bikes welcome on board","A quiet corner on the train","Parking at the edge of town"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C 讲站台实时显示屏和手机应用提醒到站时间，与前文的 knowing when 对应，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions measures that make public transport cheaper?","options":["Cheaper tickets for everyone","Buses after midnight","Knowing when the bus will come","Bikes welcome on board","A quiet corner on the train","Parking at the edge of town"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A 说公交票价下调三分之一、周末学生和老人免费，还推出九十分钟联票，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions facilities for people who combine cycling with public transport?","options":["Cheaper tickets for everyone","Buses after midnight","Knowing when the bus will come","Bikes welcome on board","A quiet corner on the train","Parking at the edge of town"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D 说新公交车装有可放两辆自行车的架子，部分列车设挂钩和充电点，方便骑行加公共交通的出行方式，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a service for people who finish work late at night?","options":["Cheaper tickets for everyone","Buses after midnight","Knowing when the bus will come","Bikes welcome on board","A quiet corner on the train","Parking at the edge of town"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B 说过去末班车十点半就收车，护士、清洁工和餐馆员工只能打车，如今周五周六试点夜班车到凌晨一点，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions a change that allows passengers to rest or work during the journey?","options":["Cheaper tickets for everyone","Buses after midnight","Knowing when the bus will come","Bikes welcome on board","A quiet corner on the train","Parking at the edge of town"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph E 讲列车设置安静车厢，禁止通话、减少广播、调暗灯光，供乘客工作、阅读或休息，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do many psychologists now believe about boredom?","options":["It is mainly caused by poor planning.","It can be valuable to the mind.","It should be treated with coffee.","It is a sign of mental illness."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第一段说越来越多心理学家认为无聊根本不是敌人，甚至可能有用，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the telephone-directory experiment find?","options":["Bored people disliked the creativity test.","The control group was more creative.","Boredom made people feel tired quickly.","Bored people produced more original ideas."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段说抄号码簿而无聊的一组在随后的创造力测试中提出的原创想法远多于对照组，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the author compare boredom to?","options":["A game played on a phone.","A failure of daily planning.","A signal such as hunger.","A cup of coffee in the morning."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段把无聊比作饥饿：饥饿提醒我们进食，无聊提醒我们正在做的事没有意义，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do some teachers ask their students to do?","options":["Leave their phones at home for a day.","Spend more time playing computer games.","Copy numbers from a directory every day.","Stop doing homework in the evening."],"answer":"A","explanation":"最后一段说一些老师要求学生一整天把手机留在家里，学生反映最初不适应，但交谈变长、作业完成得更快，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What advantage of street food does the author mention?","options":["It requires very little money to start.","It always tastes better than restaurant food.","It is only sold late at night.","It is easy for inspectors to check."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说对小微经营者而言，街头小吃让他们能用很少的本钱开始做生意，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What usually happens when cities ban street selling?","options":["Street food becomes much safer.","Vendors move to less visible places.","Prices fall in the city centre.","Customers stop eating street food."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段说全面禁止通常把摊贩逼到更不显眼的地方，那里的卫生条件可能更差，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does the new approach give vendors?","options":["Free advertising on local radio.","A larger restaurant in the city centre.","A fixed pitch with clean water and rubbish collection.","Tax-free income for the first year."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段说城市给摊贩固定摊位、提供清洁用水和垃圾清运，条件是交纳少量费用并遵守基本规则，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"26. A ______ number of studies suggests that writing notes by hand may help learners understand better.","options":[],"answer":"growing","explanation":"a growing number of 是常用搭配，意为越来越多的，说明相关研究不断增多。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"27. The typed notes were almost ______, because students could type faster.","options":[],"answer":"complete","explanation":"此处需要形容词作表语，complete 意为完整的；打字快，所以记录几乎不遗漏。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"28. A week later, the handwritten group performed ______, remembering more details of the lecture.","options":[],"answer":"better","explanation":"Yet 引出转折：虽然打字的笔记更完整，一周后测试中手写组却表现更好；better 作副词修饰 performed。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"29. The handwritten group remembered more of the ______ points.","options":[],"answer":"key","explanation":"key points 表示要点，与上文 must decide what matters 相呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"30. Writing by hand is slower, so students must ______ what matters.","options":[],"answer":"decide","explanation":"情态动词 must 后接动词原形，decide 意为决定，说明手写要求判断哪些内容重要。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"31. Students cannot copy every word, which forces them to ______ what they hear in their own words.","options":[],"answer":"summarise","explanation":"summarise 意为概括；把听到的内容用自己的话概括，与 cannot copy every word 构成因果。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"32. Typing, by contrast, encourages a ______ of transcription.","options":[],"answer":"kind","explanation":"a kind of 表示一种，此处指打字容易变成一种不加思考的照抄。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"33. The words pass from the lecturer's mouth into the notebook without ever being ______.","options":[],"answer":"understood","explanation":"being 后接过去分词构成被动，understood 意为被理解，说明照抄时信息未经加工。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"34. For lectures, students may ______ more if they put the keyboard away.","options":[],"answer":"benefit","explanation":"may 后接动词原形，benefit 意为受益，与后文 use a pen instead 的建议一致。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"blank","stem":"35. Many students also find it helpful to ______ a pen instead of a keyboard.","options":[],"answer":"use","explanation":"此处需要动词原形，use a pen 与 instead of a keyboard 形成对比，建议学生改用纸笔。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions online guides that help students find materials for their subject?","options":["Peer tutoring in the library","Study rooms open until midnight","Subject guides on the library website","Equipment training for researchers","Careers advice from the first year","Small research grants for students"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Paragraph C 介绍图书馆网站上的学科指南，列数据库、期刊和检索视频，与题干中的 online guides 对应，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions free help given by senior students in the library?","options":["Peer tutoring in the library","Study rooms open until midnight","Subject guides on the library website","Equipment training for researchers","Careers advice from the first year","Small research grants for students"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Paragraph A 说高年级学生在图书馆为一年级学生提供免费辅导，即 peer tutoring，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions training that students must complete before using research equipment?","options":["Peer tutoring in the library","Study rooms open until midnight","Subject guides on the library website","Equipment training for researchers","Careers advice from the first year","Small research grants for students"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Paragraph D 说使用科学楼研究设备前必须参加半天培训，培训后发卡才能进实验室，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions rooms that can be booked for group discussion?","options":["Peer tutoring in the library","Study rooms open until midnight","Subject guides on the library website","Equipment training for researchers","Careers advice from the first year","Small research grants for students"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Paragraph B 说三个自习室可供网上预约，配有活动桌和网络，专为小组讨论设计，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"Which paragraph mentions advice on careers offered from the first year?","options":["Peer tutoring in the library","Study rooms open until midnight","Subject guides on the library website","Equipment training for researchers","Careers advice from the first year","Small research grants for students"],"answer":"E","explanation":"Paragraph E 说就业服务中心为一年级学生开设简历、实习和与雇主交流的讲座，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What do people who multitask often believe about themselves?","options":["They finish every task more quickly.","They are good at handling several tasks at once.","They remember more than other people.","They need fewer breaks than others."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第一段说同时做几件事的人常自称擅长多任务处理，研究却给出不同结论，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the study on reading and messages find?","options":["Short messages improved students' memory of the text.","Quiet students read more slowly than others.","Students ignored most of the messages they received.","Students took longer to finish and remembered less."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第二段说边读边收短信的一组比安静组多花约四分之一时间，且记住的内容更少，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does attention residue mean?","options":["The tiredness caused by long hours of reading.","The habit of checking messages late at night.","Part of the mind staying on a previous task.","A technique for taking better notes in class."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段把 attention residue 解释为放下手机后仍有一部分注意力留在刚才的对话上，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What solution does the text suggest?","options":["Keep phones away or turn off notifications.","Stop using technology for studying entirely.","Study only in the early morning.","Read the same chapter twice every day."],"answer":"A","explanation":"最后一段说把手机放到另一个房间或关闭一小时通知，学生并没有太大困难，反而更快完成任务，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the fifteen-minute city?","options":["A city where daily needs are within a short walk or cycle.","A city with fifteen parks in every district.","A city where trains arrive every fifteen minutes.","A city that bans cars from its centre completely."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说十五分钟城市指步行或骑车一刻钟内能到达商店、学校、诊所和公园等日常所需之处，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) Which benefit of the idea is mentioned in the passage?","options":["Property prices fall in local areas.","Local shops keep more customers.","People spend less time at home.","Public transport becomes free of charge."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第二段说居民每天经过，小店铺才得以存活，即本地商店能留住更多顾客，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2025-12","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) Why is the idea hard to apply to existing cities?","options":["Residents refuse to walk anywhere.","There are not enough urban planners.","Shops and jobs are often located far away.","Walking is impossible in cold weather."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第三段说商店可能已搬到城郊的大型购物中心，上班族也可能住得很远，因此规划者提出本地中心网络，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"26. A good supervisor will ask new students to ______ everything they do, from the temperature of a water bath to the strange noise a machine makes.","options":[],"answer":"document","explanation":"document 意为「记录、存档」，document everything they do 即把所做的一切记录下来，与后文 keeping a careful record 呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"27. Keeping a careful record may feel boring, but it is the only way to ______ an unexpected result later.","options":[],"answer":"explain","explanation":"explain 意为「解释」，此处指只有记录清楚，日后才能解释异常结果出现的原因。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"28. A problem that seems unusual to one student may be ______ to someone else.","options":[],"answer":"familiar","explanation":"be familiar to sb 意为「某人对……熟悉」，a problem may be familiar to someone else 指别人可能早已遇到过这个问题。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"29. With time, students learn to ______ their methods when the data do not make sense.","options":[],"answer":"adjust","explanation":"adjust 意为「调整」，adjust their methods 指数据说不通时调整实验方法。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"30. Asking a short question early can ______ hours of wasted work.","options":[],"answer":"prevent","explanation":"prevent 意为「防止、避免」，prevent hours of wasted work 即避免浪费数小时的工作。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c6","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"31. Some measurements need weeks before any pattern becomes ______.","options":[],"answer":"obvious","explanation":"obvious 意为「明显的」，pattern becomes obvious 指规律变得清晰可见，需要数周的测量。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c7","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"32. For those who stay, the work brings a quiet ______ that no single finished result can offer.","options":[],"answer":"satisfaction","explanation":"satisfaction 为名词「满足感」，a quiet satisfaction 指那种安静的满足，与单个实验结果形成对照。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c8","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"33. Many students say the experience ______ their idea of what science really is.","options":[],"answer":"changed","explanation":"changed 是 change 的过去式，the experience changed their idea 指这段经历改变了他们对科学的认识。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c9","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"34. The most valuable habit in a lab is not speed but ______.","options":[],"answer":"patience","explanation":"patience 为名词「耐心」，not speed but patience 与前文 Patience is another lesson 呼应。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-cloze-c10","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"blank","stem":"35. By the end of a semester, most of them can ______ their own small project from start to finish.","options":[],"answer":"complete","explanation":"complete 意为「完成」，complete a project from start to finish 指独立完成一个小项目。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-matching-m1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"The festival was first suggested by a small number of students.","options":["An idea that started small","Talks that anyone could follow","Hands-on workshops","The student volunteers","Money matters","Looking ahead"],"answer":"A","explanation":"A 段说 Three second-year students were tired of hearing that science was only for people in white coats，于是向学生会申请一间小房间，说明活动源于三位学生的小想法，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-matching-m2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"Speakers are trained to avoid words their audience may not know.","options":["An idea that started small","Talks that anyone could follow","Hands-on workshops","The student volunteers","Money matters","Looking ahead"],"answer":"B","explanation":"B 段说每位讲者都要 imagine that your audience has never studied your subject，志愿者还会指出 every technical term that needs translating，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-matching-m3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"Visitors are invited to try experiments for themselves.","options":["An idea that started small","Talks that anyone could follow","Hands-on workshops","The student volunteers","Money matters","Looking ahead"],"answer":"C","explanation":"C 段说 In the workshops, visitors build simple circuits, look at cells under a microscope and test the strength of paper bridges，即让参观者亲自动手做实验，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-matching-m4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"The event is supported by students who give their time without payment.","options":["An idea that started small","Talks that anyone could follow","Hands-on workshops","The student volunteers","Money matters","Looking ahead"],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 段说 More than eighty students take part in running the event，且 None of them is paid，即八十多名学生无报酬地投入时间，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-matching-m5","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"The organisers raise funds but keep entry free.","options":["An idea that started small","Talks that anyone could follow","Hands-on workshops","The student volunteers","Money matters","Looking ahead"],"answer":"E","explanation":"E 段说 organizers apply for small grants，borrow equipment and ask local businesses for support，但 Tickets, however, stay free，即筹钱但保持免费入场，故选 E。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r1_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What caused the science magazine to nearly close?","options":["Professors stopped writing for it.","It lost its editor and faced higher printing costs.","The student union cut its budget.","Readers complained about its length."],"answer":"B","explanation":"第一段说 Its editor had graduated, the printer had raised its prices, and only four students remained，即编辑毕业、印刷涨价、人手只剩四人，故选 B。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r1_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the team change about the magazine?","options":["They began publishing short articles by undergraduates about campus research.","They moved the magazine online.","They invited professors to write longer articles.","They started a second journal."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第一段说 They stopped printing long articles written by professors and began to publish short pieces by undergraduates about research taking place on campus，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r1_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What did the chemistry student learn from writing her article?","options":["How to run a laboratory experiment.","How to use a printing machine.","How hard it is to explain research clearly.","How to become an editor."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段说她的一段文字改了五稿才让室友读懂，并说这段经历比任何写作课都有用，即体会到把研究讲清楚很难，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r1_4","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 1) What does the editor say is the purpose of the magazine?","options":["To record scientific results.","To train professional writers.","To make money for the student union.","To start conversations among readers."],"answer":"D","explanation":"第三段说 Its purpose is not to record science but to start conversations，并希望读者读完后有问题想问旁边的人，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r2_1","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What is the Academic Etiquette course mainly about?","options":["Teaching international students English grammar.","Helping students pass their final exams.","Explaining the unwritten rules of university life.","Training professors to write better emails."],"answer":"C","explanation":"第一段说 its purpose is to explain the unwritten rules of university life，并列举称呼教授、加入讨论、写邮件等，故选 C。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r2_2","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What do the two common email versions share?","options":["Both make the real request hard to find.","Both begin with a clear subject line.","Both are written in very short sentences.","Both are sent to the wrong professor."],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段说一种邮件没有称呼和主题、一口气问五个问题，另一种先长篇道歉、把真正的请求放到最后一句，共同点是请求很难被找到，故选 A。","source":"exam-practice"},
{"id":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06-reading-r2_3","paperId":"src-exam-cet4-real-2026-06","type":"single","stem":"(Passage 2) What does the teacher believe is the deeper lesson?","options":["Professors should reply to emails faster.","Language is not important in academic emails.","Students should stop asking so many questions.","Understanding academic culture is part of learning a subject."],"answer":"D","explanation":"末段说 the deeper lesson is not about language，而是理解 academic life has its own culture，学会读懂这种文化本身就是学习这门学科的一部分，故选 D。","source":"exam-practice"}
];
